f 17592186681531} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd1fa731-f650-5818-90c6-9fe7dd5e4f18" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681551} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b28caf8f-6da8-5bb3-b549-a45c0a37a9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681529} {:answer/id #uuid "d8cb12e0-f4e8-5e18-bce9-d843bc3aaca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681562} {:answer/id #uuid "e921f243-5171-5d45-96a3-7a47ab0edd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681576} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5ccaad-10df-53a0-96d1-73f33b496f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681596}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186681596} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbdcc9d7-9dbe-5f13-b544-cda2df713482" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ed6baa52-f8a6-585c-af0d-113506006f16" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an **integrating factor** to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'=- 6 y - 5\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. Take $x$ to be real.\n\nFor a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes $e^x$.\n\nFirst, rewrite our equation in differential form:\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=6 y + 5,\\qquad Q(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nNow compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}={{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}={{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-7}}\n$$\n\nand multiplication gives the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$.\n\nHere $\\exp(x)=e^x$.\n\nFirst, rewrite our equation in differential form:\n\n$$\n(6 y + 5)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=6 y + 5,\\qquad Q(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nCompute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(6 y + 5)=6 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})&=\\frac{1}{1}(6-(0)) \\\\ &=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a function of $x$ only. Choose the multiplicative constant to be one. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x)&=\\exp(\\int (6)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ &=\\exp(6 x) \\\\ &=e^{6 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the original differential equation by this integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x)((6 y + 5)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y)&=I(x)\\cdot 0 \\\\ e^{6 x}((6 y + 5)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "ef5d7c42-7a56-5cff-82c8-2d447ef49443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681585} {:answer/id #uuid "f430022f-ddf9-5fbc-9926-532e91d7db10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})" :db/id #edb/ref 175921