 :db/id #edb/ref 17592186691820} {:answer/id #uuid "92d4a8a4-a180-5476-a270-95bbe58cd3c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{2 x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186691843} {:answer/id #uuid "b4941fd6-4ce4-562d-8a8e-6a24065f6d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{- x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186691857}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186691820} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff8b83fc-5d59-555b-bf76-4fa678f92caa" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186691888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7fbcc1dc-dd71-5f72-971f-7c5b6b7f10ec" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an **integrating factor** to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n(- 4 x y)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. Take $x$ to be real.\n\nFor a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes $e^x$.\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=- 4 x y,\\qquad Q(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nNow compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}={{field-4}}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})={{field-5}}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand multiplication gives the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}\n$$\n\nChoose the multiplicative constant to be one." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$.\n\nHere $\\exp(x)=e^x$.\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=- 4 x y,\\qquad Q(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nCompute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- 4 x y)=- 4 x \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})&=\\frac{1}{1}(- 4 x-(0)) \\\\ &=- 4 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a function of $x$ only. Choose the multiplicative constant to be one. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x)&=\\exp(\\int (- 4 x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ &=\\exp(- 2 x^{2}) \\\\ &=e^{- 2 x^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the original differential equation by this integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x)((- 4 x y)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y)&=I(x)\\cdot 0 \\\\ e^{- 2 x^{2}}((- 4 x y)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "995986ff-25c6-5c7d-aca0-78d72253a17c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186691849} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd8d77d-9b57-513f-bff0-00f5fd834933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186691850} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a38d56-85ba-56a8-a986-956439dda9a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186691859} {:answer/id #uuid "b8714cd6-90f0-5a6d-b7da-7ee0662402fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186691860} {:answer/id #uuid "c332080c-5199-539f-9bd2-d7c2d071a996" :answer/ty