{3}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\frac{x}{2}$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nNormalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}=2\\sqrt{1-(\\frac{x}{2})^2}.\n$$\n\nThe differential is $\\mathrm{d}x=2\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. The outside factor is $\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2=3$.\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{1}{2}\\\\x=\\sqrt{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=3\\left(\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEndpoint values: $\\arcsin{\\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\right)}=\\frac{\\pi}{6},\\quad \\arcsin{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)}=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$.\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\arcsin{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the primitive and subtract lower from upper:\n\n$$\n3\\left(\\left[\\arcsin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}\\right)=3\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nThe exact answer is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "524fb952-2eab-5a48-87d8-266780ca5971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693086}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693086} :answer-field/id #uuid "8872060e-15bf-590a-bf77-9b4df5106182" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9fa48c8e-d2b6-5eba-8ad3-f816664db3eb" :question/problem "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{25 - x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\frac{x}{5}$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nNormalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{25 - x^{2}}=5\\sqrt{1-(\\frac{x}{5})^2}.\n$$\n\nThe differential is $\\mathrm{d}x=5\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. The outside factor is $\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot 5=4$.\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=0\\\\x=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{25 - x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=4\\left(\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEndpoint values: $\\arcsin{\\left(0 \\right)}=0,\\quad \\arcsin{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)}=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$.\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\arcsin{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the primitive and subtract lower from upper:\n\n$$\n4\\left(\\left[\\arcsin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}\\right)=4\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nThe exact answer is\n\n$$\n\\pi.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ede60e48-9cdd-536f-b441-48e9615792ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693148}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693148} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e0a536-3443-5d5d-926c-ef349b9c9b62" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b5286232-dcfd-5ef5-b3e2-5f7420d2ca2f" :question/problem "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{-4}^{4} \\frac{2}{x^{2} + 16}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\frac{x}{4}$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nNormalize the denominator:\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 16=16\\left(1+(\\frac{x}{4})^2\\right).\n$$\n\nThe differential is $\\mathrm{d}x=4\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. The outside factor is $\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-4&\\Longrightarrow u=-1\\\\x=4&\\Longrightarrow u=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{-4}^{4} \\frac{2}{x^{2} + 16}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int_{-1}^{1} \\frac{1}{u^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEndpoint values: $\\arctan{\\left(-1 \\right)}=- \\frac{\\pi}{4},\\quad \\arctan{\\left(1 \\right)}=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$.\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{u^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\arctan{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the primitive and subtract lower from upper:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\left[\\arctan{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{-1}^{1}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nThe exact answer is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57a22543-28d5-5089-8691-a57799840d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693088}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693088} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f2fd66c-c278-5547-a2e0-a6c78f6e834b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba24a3c5-0af8-5f12-bad0-104365814844" :question/problem "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\frac{2}{x^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\frac{x}{3}$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nNormalize the denominator:\n\