d #edb/ref 17592186705787}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705779} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ce223d4-9b13-58b3-9ebf-d185b940aafa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b46a9989-787a-50e7-9439-e3b7e0e7b866" :question/problem "${3}^{-3}=$" :question/worked-solution "We find the reciprocal of the base $(3),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(+3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{-3} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3})\\times (\\frac{1}{3})\\times (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1\\times 1\\times 1}{3\\times 3\\times 3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20844911-f1dc-56ad-922a-208a14d6066a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705825} {:answer/id #uuid "8538dfdc-f160-599e-a5d9-58fe63a6e3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705839} {:answer/id #uuid "ad13460b-3c28-52ad-bad9-21121f2efd95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705842} {:answer/id #uuid "bec1e64c-0ada-5acb-a9fc-aa1d7f963204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705846} {:answer/id #uuid "e0cff523-d3ed-5463-a055-9a8016b6a7b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705852}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705846} :answer-field/id #uuid "c15a75c9-1103-5a89-bd9d-8ebe29e9b9ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b6486b02-9fbd-5750-809b-8d4c1bc07b11" :question/problem "The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $\\mathbf{a}_2$ if\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Because $\\mathbf{a}_1$ is nonzero, we need **one common scalar** $c$ such that $\\mathbf{a}_2=c\\mathbf{a}_1$ in every coordinate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}_2 &=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)(-12) \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)(-6) \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)(-6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\mathbf{a}_1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other choices fail a coordinate check:\n\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{6}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{3}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{3}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{3}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n\nRearrange the scalar-multiple equality:\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\mathbf{a}_1-\\mathbf{a}_2=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\nThus $(x_1,x_2)=(\\frac{1}{3},-1)$ solves $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2=\\mathbf{0}$. These coefficients are **not both zero**, so the set is linearly dependent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22c6349a-003f-5bc9-b82d-aeccc2303824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705826} {:answer/id #uuid "4c1bc4f6-277e-5cb8-8239-944f9bcf9ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705830} {:answer/id #uuid "63aa5aae-931a-5018-91dc-accd70f3123c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705832} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3b4b1c-b1ee-54d0-adf2-1040c678c8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705834} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebee206-cbc8-5917-a749-fc15d1b2c17c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705835}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705832} :answer-field/id #uuid "e222c5e1-fae1-5df5-bb28-076892716f40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba91be3b-bde0-5aed-b962-90d0afe37a87" :question/problem "The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $\\mathbf{a}_2$ if\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Because $\\mathbf{a}_1$ is nonzero, we need **one common scalar** $c$ such that $\\mathbf{a}_2=c\\mathbf{a}_1$ in every coordinate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}_2 &=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(-4) \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(-4) \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(-4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\mathbf{a}_1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other choices fail a coordinate check:\n\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $1$ and $\\frac{1}{2}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\frac{1}{2}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $1$ in coordinates $1$ and $3$, respectively. They disagree.\n\nRearrange the scalar-multiple equality:\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\mathbf{a}_1-\\mathbf{a}_2=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\nThus $(x_1,x_2)=(\\frac{1}{2},-1)$ solves $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2=\\mathbf{0}$. These coefficients are **not both zero**, so the set is linearly dependent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b01eec7-0118-5c75-b207-f9f3c216d7b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705833} {:answer/id #uuid "7515f5fa-c028-5855-8025-bda87098a8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705837} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ae1fdd-0e8c-5f40-8426-13120cb6653e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705840} {:answer/id #uuid "ab607c71-f011-590f-8e72-06be83fdd0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705841} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ad239b-0b8a-5b88-868b-9977efa070c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705854}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705837} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0974abd-14f6-5f86-9497-8e6c75fa7e24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f0b2e9da-054c-5a8c-9aad-523a2a909087" :question/problem "The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $\\mathbf{a}_2$ if\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Because $\\mathbf{a}_1$ is nonzero, we need **one common scalar** $c$ such that $\\mathbf{a}_2=c\\mathbf{a}_1$ in every coordinate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}_2 &=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(4) \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(4) \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(8)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &= \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\mathbf{a}_1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other choices fail a coordinate check:\n\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $-\\frac{1}{4}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $-1$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $-\\frac{1}{4}$ in coordinates $1$ and $3$, respectively. They disagree.\n\nRearrange the scalar-multiple equality:\n$$\n\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\mathbf{a}_1-\\mathbf{a}_2=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\nThus $(x_1,x_2)=(-\\frac{1}{2},-1)$ solves $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2=\\mathbf{0}$. These coefficients are **not both zero**, so the set is linearly dependent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1165c7a7-0046-5b80-939d-c391cd809b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705823} {:answer/id #uuid "50b6129c-c2d0-5dee-a8db-d551eb47f908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705831} {:answer/id #uuid "6efc2d27-90ef-539e-b98a-1946b241aeb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705836} {:answer/id #uuid "7aab151a-b909-58bc-bb5b-654a7bc22274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705838} {:answer/id #uuid "b8919f73-d0c1-5620-bb83-fc818fb9c197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705844}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705823} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b4f7035-6ffe-53a9-80f6-a0f23de30f75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f115bd60-c202-53c7-8d2b-bfa3872c0f75" :question/problem "The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $\\mathbf{a}_2$ if\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Because $\\mathbf{a}_1$ is nonzero, we need **one common scalar** $c$ such that $\\mathbf{a}_2=c\\mathbf{a}_1$ in every coordinate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}_2 &=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)(-6) \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)(6) \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)(6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &= \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\mathbf{a}_1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other choices fail a coordinate check:\n\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $-\\frac{1}{6}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $\\frac{2}{3}$ and $-\\frac{1}{3}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $\\frac{1}{3}$ and $-\\frac{1}{3}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $\\frac{1}{3}$ and $-\\frac{1}{3}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n\nRearrange the scalar-multiple equality:\n$$\n\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\mathbf{a}_1-\\mathbf{a}_2=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\nThus $(x_1,x_2)=(-\\frac{1}{3},-1)$ solves $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2=\\mathbf{0}$. These coefficients are **not both zero**, so the set is linearly dependent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23e099b2-1c11-5d0e-a74a-ba5e1881d6f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{4 \\left(2 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)} + y\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\frac{2 \\left(- 2 x + y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705862} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb8dae2-129d-5a09-b4f1-ac3faf9aa29e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{4 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\frac{y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705864} {:answer/id #uuid "31442dbe-31b7-52e0-8935-1a29bdc7241e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{2 \\left(4 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)} - y\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\frac{2 \\left(x + y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705865} {:answer/id #uuid "74a6e24c-ca10-5ba8-82b3-f66ed2bd70d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{2 \\left(2 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)} + y\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\frac{- 2 x + y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705875} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb196cf-e98b-5801-b008-67288bd48e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{4 \\left(- x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)} + y\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}- \\frac{4 x + y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705887}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705875} :answer-field/id #uuid "05b25d21-a333-5a94-b45a-4cb6160fff11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c2cbd5c6-85bc-5dd6-8e34-a08261f0b986" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}, \\\\ \\nabla f&=\\frac{2 y}{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}\\mathbf i- \\frac{2 x}{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}\\mathbf j \\\\ g(x,y)&=\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}, \\\\ \\nabla g&=\\frac{4 x}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssume $y\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Use the **product rule**: $\\nabla(fg)=g\\nabla f+f\\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.\n\nNow collect each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\\\ &=(\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}})\\left(\\frac{2 y}{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad+(\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)})\\left(\\frac{4 x}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{2 y}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}} \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(\\frac{4 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\left(2 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)} + y\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\\\ &=(\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}})\\left(- \\frac{2 x}{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad+(\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)})\\left(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{2 x}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}} \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(\\frac{y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{- 2 x + y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{2 \\left(2 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)} + y\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\frac{- 2 x + y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the contributions to each coordinate separately."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f6c7b7d-9365-5dce-bb84-d4a19cf7cd79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705905} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f9d489-c9b2-51bf-ae43-e5b52b140cea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -18 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705916} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c42a5f-17ab-5e70-b0a2-2422d44ee633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -18 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705919} {:answer/id #uuid "ed6e2352-a77b-5726-8a1c-a641b204b566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705921} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2f5210-4f5c-59f4-8b5e-c486587f5482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -18 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705922}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705905} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae1bd8cf-b0a3-54e2-8324-6dbae183d356" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ea3ac23f-51a1-561a-b590-88e89f21b3c9" :question/problem "The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $\\mathbf{a}_2$ if\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Because $\\mathbf{a}_1$ is nonzero, we need **one common scalar** $c$ such that $\\mathbf{a}_2=c\\mathbf{a}_1$ in every coordinate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}_2 &=\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)(12) \\\\ \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)(-8) \\\\ \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)(4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\mathbf{a}_1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other choices fail a coordinate check:\n\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -18 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $1$ and $\\frac{9}{4}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -18 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\frac{9}{4}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -18 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $1$ and $\\frac{9}{4}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n- The candidate $\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}$ requires multipliers $1$ and $\\frac{3}{2}$ in coordinates $1$ and $2$, respectively. They disagree.\n\nRearrange the scalar-multiple equality:\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\mathbf{a}_1-\\mathbf{a}_2=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\nThus $(x_1,x_2)=(\\frac{3}{2},-1)$ solves $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2=\\mathbf{0}$. These coefficients are **not both zero**, so the set is linearly dependent."} {:answer/id #uuid "079d2197-2dc8-5ae0-92fd-3f51fe1a8344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},1,-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f7a428-e4b9-5cf7-804e-80af926f4040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=1\\\\y&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + 2\\\\z&=e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705924} {:answer/id #uuid "2305897b-feda-5ab8-a666-9b65a8bcd973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=1\\\\y&=2\\\\z&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705931} {:answer/id #uuid "7c431659-9a3d-52c9-81ae-36e8c598ae19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + 1\\\\y&=2\\\\z&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705944} {:answer/id #uuid "93965054-0281-5e8a-92d4-b1c228b63a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + 1\\\\y&=2\\\\z&=e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705947} {:answer/id #uuid "f071dfe1-3d71-5e03-892a-0812240a324f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + 1\\\\y&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + 2\\\\z&=e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705953}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705931} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab6e744-82e8-5f3b-8cb6-459842390076" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "117c2134-e2a8-5d37-bce5-c21f4883b2f8" :question/problem "Find parametric equations of the normal line to the surface\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ln{\\left(\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2} \\right)}\\\\\n&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nat $P(1,2,e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2)$. Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms together and set\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF(x,y,z)&=\\ln{\\left(\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2} \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=1$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{2 \\left(x - 1\\right)}{\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{2 \\left(y - 2\\right)}{\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{2 \\left(z - 2\\right)}{\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(y - 2\\right)&=0\\\\\n\\left(x - 1\\right)&=0\\\\\n\\left(z - 2\\right)&=e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these values into the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(P)&=\\frac{2 \\left(0\\right)}{\\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)^{2}}\\\\\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(P)&=\\frac{2 \\left(0\\right)}{\\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)^{2}}\\\\\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(P)&=\\frac{2 \\left(e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)}{\\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)^{2}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\nabla F(1,2,e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2)=\\langle 0,0,\\frac{2}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}\\rangle\\ne\\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.\n\nMultiply every component by the same nonzero scalar $\\frac{1}{2}$ to obtain the equivalent direction\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n=\\frac{1}{2}\\langle 0,0,\\frac{2}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}\\rangle=\\langle 0,0,e^{- \\frac{1}{2}}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,2,e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\rangle+t\\,\\langle 0,0,e^{- \\frac{1}{2}}\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nEquating coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=1\\\\y&=2\\\\z&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "1f24dd21-13b7-5c00-9363-ad415328a6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},-1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705930} {:answer/id #uuid "2cfc4d03-e143-5cc4-b9bf-35a1ea97a3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},- \\frac{1}{2}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705933} {:answer/id #uuid "2db4ac74-b16f-58a7-a971-8f8d0742b200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705934} {:answer/id #uuid "2faa4958-e3ce-5f03-a110-35a2c12d0846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705935} {:answer/id #uuid "3c18f78a-eb67-5bab-83c2-c6bc2398d3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705936} {:answer/id #uuid "493e584a-9cac-5253-ae91-5754e08d4eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},0,-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079d2197-2dc8-5ae0-92fd-3f51fe1a8344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},1,-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705923} {:answer/id #uuid "2db4ac74-b16f-58a7-a971-8f8d0742b200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705934} {:answer/id #uuid "3c18f78a-eb67-5bab-83c2-c6bc2398d3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705936} {:answer/id #uuid "493e584a-9cac-5253-ae91-5754e08d4eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},0,-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705938} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0d46fe-50b2-5c2f-b5dc-8c4aff625202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},-1,-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705934} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ca93aa0-504e-5847-b36f-29319e32416c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5b821f7d-0519-503a-8177-317da9e0701d" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n&\\left(y - 2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(- x - 2\\right)\\\\\n&{}+2 \\left(- x - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+2 \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nat $P(- \\frac{5}{2},5,0)$. Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms together and set\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF(x,y,z)&=\\left(y - 2\\right)\\\\\n&{}-\\left(- x - 2\\right)\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(- x - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=0$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(x,y,z)&=1\\\\\n&{}+4 \\left(- x - 2\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(x,y,z)&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(x,y,z)&=-4 \\left(z + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(- x - 2\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\\\\n\\left(z + 1\\right)&=1\\\\\n\\left(y - 2\\right)&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these values into the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(P)&=1\\\\\n&{}+4 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(P)&=1\\\\\n&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(P)&=-4 \\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\nabla F(- \\frac{5}{2},5,0)=\\langle 3,1,-4\\rangle\\ne\\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.\n\nMultiply every component by the same nonzero scalar $\\frac{1}{2}$ to obtain the equivalent direction\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n=\\frac{1}{2}\\langle 3,1,-4\\rangle=\\langle \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},-2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},-2\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc52d61-7f78-5bc3-8bb0-db3e2a26165e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},0\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705946} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0d46fe-50b2-5c2f-b5dc-8c4aff625202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},-1,-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f24dd21-13b7-5c00-9363-ad415328a6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},-1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705930} {:answer/id #uuid "2cfc4d03-e143-5cc4-b9bf-35a1ea97a3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},- \\frac{1}{2}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705933} {:answer/id #uuid "2faa4958-e3ce-5f03-a110-35a2c12d0846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705935} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc52d61-7f78-5bc3-8bb0-db3e2a26165e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},0\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705946} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3d335d-5b9c-5bd1-bdc8-ab2e421959fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705935} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c37b48c-76ce-5f9c-b13d-e9f3e5da6d74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "faa8cbcc-cc2d-5db3-9bae-d21e162768f5" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ln{\\left(\\left(- y - 2\\right) + x + z \\right)}\\\\\n&=-\\left(- y - 2\\right) x z\\\\\n&{}+\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nat $P(0,-3,1)$. Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms together and set\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF(x,y,z)&=\\left(- y - 2\\right) x z\\\\\n&{}+\\ln{\\left(\\left(- y - 2\\right) + x + z \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{1}{\\left(- y - 2\\right) + x + z}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(- y - 2\\right) z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(x,y,z)&=-\\frac{1}{\\left(- y - 2\\right) + x + z}\\\\\n&{}-x z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{1}{\\left(- y - 2\\right) + x + z}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(- y - 2\\right) x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(- y - 2\\right)&=1\\\\\nx&=0\\\\\nz&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these values into the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(P)&=\\frac{1}{\\left(1\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(1\\right)}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(P)&=-\\frac{1}{\\left(1\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(1\\right)}\\\\\n&{}-\\left(0\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(P)&=\\frac{1}{\\left(1\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(1\\right)}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(1\\right) \\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\nabla F(0,-3,1)=\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\rangle\\ne\\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.\n\nThe point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3d335d-5b9c-5bd1-bdc8-ab2e421959fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6300415b-df5d-520a-80af-47c4f4fb9575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{5} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707239} {:answer/id #uuid "85aac80b-0592-5169-b5f8-442f1cfc1e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707245} {:answer/id #uuid "92b7b410-e194-5dea-b875-e93ea48557ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707249} {:answer/id #uuid "aca8069b-c814-508e-84e6-2ce3d7de5371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}r^{4} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707252} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d465f7-45b6-5279-9486-7060448da862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{4} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707254} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d20398a-4cb8-570f-826f-01b6be290d1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "01bf462a-11ba-5f3b-9c84-759ffcb8189e" :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):x^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2}\\leq\\frac{1}{4}\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The full disk has radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ and center $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$.\n\n**Radial bounds.** Expanding the disk inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2-1y&\\leq0\\\\r^2-1r\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}&\\leq0\\\\r(r-(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}))&\\leq0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$, divide by $r$; the origin is included as well. Thus $0\\leq r\\leq \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$.\n\n**Angular bounds.** The upper radial bound must be nonnegative. The full disk permits $0\\leq\\theta\\leq\\pi$. Choose $0\\leq\\theta\\leq\\pi$. Every interior point is covered once.\n\n![Region, representative radial segment and angular sweep](images/4c/4c9c4c04113e0e8c940bca28430d29116ca0273b7523a293bc53560e42d11f4b.png)\n\n**Transformed integrand.** The density substitution and area factor give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x y^{2}&=r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\\\x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=r^{4} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{4} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n**Other choices.** $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{5} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The area factor was squared instead of used once. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. The area factor is missing. $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}r^{4} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. The area factor is missing."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013788f2-f22f-59ee-aa38-5256f270e37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707229} {:answer/id #uuid "3050a952-0be4-5f11-9a6f-10b5783c865c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707233} {:answer/id #uuid "a7bae241-f2b6-59ff-8adf-28c3ea5321a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707250} {:answer/id #uuid "f14190dc-b26f-526d-86dc-9cca5016e72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707257} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b60e7a-8bd1-59f1-88d7-bf345b67c5f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707258}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707257} :answer-field/id #uuid "830f000a-3a67-557f-bc33-244cadf2dbee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8ffe86dc-71ff-544d-98c3-22e21773caac" :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):x^{2}+(y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}\\leq\\frac{9}{4}\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x^{2} - y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The full disk has radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ and center $(0,- \\frac{3}{2})$.\n\n**Radial bounds.** Expanding the disk inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2+3y&\\leq0\\\\r^2+3r\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}&\\leq0\\\\r(r-(- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}))&\\leq0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$, divide by $r$; the origin is included as well. Thus $0\\leq r\\leq - 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$.\n\n**Angular bounds.** The upper radial bound must be nonnegative. The full disk permits $\\pi\\leq\\theta\\leq2 \\pi$. Choose $\\pi\\leq\\theta\\leq2 \\pi$. Every interior point is covered once.\n\n![Region, representative radial segment and angular sweep](images/af/af753b03aa012acb3b40fc8b1fe651b21033f2aebf214015f95dd3c6669457e3.png)\n\n**Transformed integrand.** Use $\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta=\\cos(2\\theta)$ in the difference of squares. The density substitution and area factor give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2} - y^{2}&=- r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\\\&=r^{2} \\left(- \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\\\x^{2} - y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n**Other choices.** $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The area factor was divided out instead of multiplied in. $\\displaystyle\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. The area factor is missing. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. The area factor was divided out instead of multiplied in."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707253 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0102cc08-e057-55e3-a286-f360b202f110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707227} {:answer/id #uuid "41e02736-b465-5ae2-89bd-091c8b798f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707235} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6d6f05-025a-56af-9a63-b1bac2935f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707242} {:answer/id #uuid "a99a2148-a21c-564a-b10f-62d4baa5a35c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707251} {:answer/id #uuid "e4efbbbe-df15-5ea7-8068-36c27345970c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707256}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707235} :answer-field/id #uuid "011a7a5e-d126-5aeb-888a-a15fef263f31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b4f0f3bc-82bd-563b-84cb-90c42857caf0" :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):(x+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}+y^{2}\\leq\\frac{1}{4}\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The full disk has radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ and center $(- \\frac{1}{2},0)$.\n\n**Radial bounds.** Expanding the disk inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2+1x&\\leq0\\\\r^2+1r\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}&\\leq0\\\\r(r-(- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}))&\\leq0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$, divide by $r$; the origin is included as well. Thus $0\\leq r\\leq - \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$.\n\n**Angular bounds.** The upper radial bound must be nonnegative. The full disk permits $\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$. Choose $\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$. Every interior point is covered once.\n\n![Region, representative radial segment and angular sweep](images/74/7422e92679515dcce0587cc6b518b259493ca13408d8be7c7f43e0dbcfd6e9a5.png)\n\n**Transformed integrand.** The density substitution and area factor give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y^{2}&=r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\\\y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n**Other choices.** $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The area factor is missing. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. The area factor was divided out instead of multiplied in. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. The area factor was divided out instead of multiplied in."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "356179d8-8cce-5049-af97-66195dc13091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707460}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707460} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d9391d-df13-57b4-91a1-2534ae8598de" :answer-field/key "slope" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "75a1ce50-36a6-5f83-b7bc-c355f8d4af52" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y)$. The curve $C$ is its intersection with the plane $y=\\pi$. Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ lies on $C$ and that\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=y\\cos x + \\cos y.\n$$\n\nFind the exact slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$, measured with respect to $x$. $m=$ {{slope}}" :question/worked-solution "The plane $y=\\pi$ holds $y$ constant, so $x$ varies and the section slope is $dz/dx=f_x(x,y)$. Substitute the ordered coordinates of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f_x(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2},\\pi)&=(\\pi)\\cos \\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)+\\cos (\\pi) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07250558-c46d-5dc0-aae7-2b4c74b870d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707475} {:answer/id #uuid "c5113b47-6c5d-5aee-a8f6-ed1202372083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707510}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707475} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc85fe1e-8e16-5eed-83b3-a583d02bb24b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707512} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "776a362e-5a5d-5402-9735-b9408b301b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707491} {:answer/id #uuid "887d6ab8-34bd-514d-9d34-35e11bb57002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707496} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd76a44a-4fca-58f7-b239-543bd79fbe87" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707513} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab46545-0f01-5656-9a5f-c139d7bf503b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707477} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7b5a55-f8a3-5ff0-99e4-a0d58d6f7dd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707508}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707477} :answer-field/id #uuid "f21d3b70-33af-5145-bdc6-3eab6b1abfe2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1e324553-d08f-5dbc-b9fd-80a83282ecb8" :question/problem "Which formulas define vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$ on their **entire stated domains**? Select all that apply. Keep every stated domain unchanged. The symbols $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j$ are the planar unit vectors.\n\n- **I.** $G(x,y)=\\ln(x+2)\\,\\mathbf i$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x+2>0\\}$.\n\n- **II.** $F(x,y)=\\langle\\sqrt{x-y+1},\\ln(x+y+3)\\rangle$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x-y+1\\geq0,\\ x+y+3\\geq0\\}$.\n\n- **III.** $V(x,y)=\\langle\\frac{1}{x+2},\\sqrt{y+1}\\rangle$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x+2\\geq0,\\ y+1\\geq0\\}$." :question/worked-solution "Check planar inputs and outputs, then test whether the formula is real and defined at every point of the stated domain.\n\n- **I.** $G$ The components require $x+2>0$. Every point in the stated domain satisfies these conditions. The omitted planar component is zero: $\\ln(x+2)\\,\\mathbf i+0\\,\\mathbf j=\\langle\\ln(x+2),0\\rangle$. Its output is still planar. The inputs and outputs are planar, so select this function.\n\n- **II.** $F$ The components require $x-y+1\\geq0,\\ x+y+3>0$. The point $(-2,-1)$ lies in the stated domain, but gives $\\ln(0)$; the logarithm is undefined. The formula therefore fails on the entire stated domain. Do not select this function; shrinking the domain is not allowed in this question.\n\n- **III.** $V$ The components require $x+2\\ne0,\\ y+1\\geq0$. The point $(-2,0)$ lies in the stated domain, but gives $1/0$; the reciprocal is undefined. The formula therefore fails on the entire stated domain. Do not select this function; shrinking the domain is not allowed in this question.\n\nSelect I only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "482d1d74-2f4a-5ab9-9281-951a0af4e8b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707483} {:answer/id #uuid "92d5b50d-f72c-5d26-b2a8-0a1c93248ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707500}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707500} :answer-field/id #uuid "34d05b28-cad7-5a10-9036-e05c69e9af36" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707480} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7506b157-34f1-55ba-b69d-6943fbc2dca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707490} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8d44b7-53a4-5eab-9e89-5f8432cbb1b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707505