er-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716523} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dbfa922-2fec-551c-8f03-128bfeef1350" :answer-field/key "point-x" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e28cfcf3-7b63-552b-8096-4cfc3d2ec7d9" :question/problem "The graph is $f(t)=\\sin t$ restricted to $[-\\pi/2,\\pi/2]$.\n\n![Restricted sine with a marked point](images/73/7300ba3edc718097f88dbfc66339000548fb45bc330a2ad5b86b8e5e89d582a5.png)\n\nReflect it in $y=x$ to obtain $y=\\arcsin x$. The marked point becomes a point with $x$-coordinate {{point-x}} and $y$-coordinate {{point-y}}. Give exact values.\n\nSelect the reflected graph. Every candidate has domain $[-1,1]$, range $[-\\pi/2,\\pi/2]$, the same endpoints, and increases through $(0,0)$. Use the marked interior point." :question/worked-solution "Reflection in $y=x$ swaps coordinates:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\pi}{6},1/2)\\longmapsto(1/2,\\frac{\\pi}{6}).\n$$\n\nThe blanks are $1/2$ and $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$. The endpoints reflect to $(-1,-\\pi/2)$ and $(1,\\pi/2)$. Endpoint and direction checks alone do not distinguish these candidates. At $x=1/2$, only the graph below has height $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$; it follows the reflected sine curve. The other graphs place the marked interior point too high or too low.\n\n![](images/c7/c73ad5138a55ca7a5a9ab57fa37dc1b0c72fa38b5b8f7744ef21ecc60cb70200.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fc20370-81cc-5aa5-aa73-d9f3f36dc575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(6 t^{3} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{2}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716655} {:answer/id #uuid "54e3da9a-d19a-57fe-8ffa-31de0dbe77f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(6 t^{3} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{6}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716666} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6b4142-4629-5edd-b091-74ba0b531476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(6 t^{2} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{2}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716671} {:answer/id #uuid "84f4f900-c4cd-5d67-9581-3729a5c67f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(6 t^{2} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{4}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716672} {:answer/id #uuid "e89650f1-4674-5bfe-a849-e63683522b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(2 t^{2} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{4}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716681}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716672} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fc1e1a9-d64b-53c1-9023-3739c87dfdf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3b5cab76-5120-5534-a8e7-a8349e068186" :question/problem "![Curve C with endpoints A and B and arrows along the path.](images/68/68d39defe7e4584c6b645163402cfbaefca793311f9849f8ee249bed57dc086e.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 2 x y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{3}{y}$ traversed between the points $(\\frac{3}{2},2)$ and $(\\frac{3}{4},4)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{3}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle \\frac{3}{t},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 2\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 4$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\right)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{t^4}+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{4} + 9}{t^4}} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{4} + 9}}{t^2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2 x y^{3},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)t^{3} \\\\ &=6 t^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{2}^{4}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{2}^{4}(6 t^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{4}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{2}^{4}6 \\sqrt{t^{4} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1efd0fce-e423-5998-b3fd-20eb4cba67d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{6}\\left(12 t^{2} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{16}{t^{4}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716654} {:answer/id #uuid "2279feaa-43b4-5a79-81a0-0008d329df78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{6}\\left(12 t \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{16}{t^{4}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716656} {:answer/id #uuid "a14ccd94-3e1d-5736-abfe-d9e732f47c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{6}\\left(12 t \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{16}{t^{6}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716674} {:answer/id #uuid "e160dc41-5742-5c89-be96-0cd29d54f8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{6}\\left(12 t^{2} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{16}{t^{2}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716679} {:answer/id #uuid "e32aea50-4820-5ec3-839e-92669e36a968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{6}\\left(3 t \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{16}{t^{4}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716680}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716656} :answer-field/id #uuid "f96d3f7e-5b5f-5bb1-ab99-737abce170d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e942296e-f9c6-5796-a875-b8567d46c2cc" :question/problem "![Curve C with endpoints A and B and arrows along the path.](images/1c/1cc539b4c2ebce9ebcc17dc39e7c9dec840fe55cb5e4162aaad5719b400f33fc.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 3 x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{4}{y}$ traversed between the points $(4,1)$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},6)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{4}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle \\frac{4}{t},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 6$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\frac{4}{t}\\right)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{4}{t^{2}}\\right)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{4}{t^{2}}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{t^4}+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{4} + 16}{t^4}} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{4} + 16}}{t^2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=3 x y^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=3\\left(\\frac{4}{t}\\right)t^{2} \\\\ &=12 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 3 x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{6}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{6}(12 t)\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{16}{t^{4}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{6}\\frac{12 \\sqrt{t^{4} + 16}}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "212c2921-d30c-57c3-bbea-b7a6621b0eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 \\sqrt{3} + 32 \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716689} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1868be-7916-5714-954c-369ca2de3a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{250}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716712} {:answer/id #uuid "da817805-7ee3-5a5a-940a-309d6c888e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716715} {:answer/id #uuid "e70e0dd7-8003-585c-a83a-be0ed35285b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{266}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716716} {:answer/id #uuid "e7977b4e-a75a-5dd8-94e4-b0c84bfb7380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{609}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716717}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716715} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6bfb20c-8eb0-5d30-b676-058d70e47a61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "34f44129-201a-54e6-970d-29d57028a73d" :question/problem "![](images/68/68d8212cddf5e8b7d6d3036582d0cbbae1b37b67e1725781a2a78e1bc8a1fd17.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C 2 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$ between $(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}{2},2 \\sqrt{6})$ and $(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{2},\\sqrt{3})$." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. The endpoint $y$-coordinates give $\\sqrt{3}\\le t\\le 2 \\sqrt{6}$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=\\langle \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},t\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{t},1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)^2+1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $t>0$, the denominator in the norm simplifies without changing sign.\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=2 t^{3} e^{- \\left(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}=2 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $e^{-\\ln t}=1/t$ because $t>0$.\n\nMultiply by the arc-length factor:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{\\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}\\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{\\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}2 t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=t^{2} + 1$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=2 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $4$ and $25$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{4}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{2 u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right]_{4}^{25} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{250}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=78\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38395fe5-ee3a-508f-9dca-d1d1bc56427f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "125 - 2 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716695} {:answer/id #uuid "6444413a-8648-5f12-81f4-c3cec79e3320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{2} - \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716700} {:answer/id #uuid "80f3c0cc-a6b6-5fa4-bc2e-967dd57598df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1 + 48 \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716703} {:answer/id #uuid "97b801e8-cdb9-5575-9672-2e1fa3c5abe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716705} {:answer/id #uuid "fd94540b-67b1-5370-8402-8f5157373521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{3} - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716695} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f846877-c953-5e54-876d-6af2265b8301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3961ef96-6834-5c4f-88b9-d15a61ff68b5" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a6f4294d412a929e06bb9d858983e171d4e0d5c422fcf9d68f809f1abd0a2439.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C 3 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$ between $(0,1)$ and $(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}{2},2 \\sqrt{6})$." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. The endpoint $y$-coordinates give $1\\le t\\le 2 \\sqrt{6}$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=\\langle \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},t\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{t},1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)^2+1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $t>0$, the denominator in the norm simplifies without changing sign.\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=3 t^{3} e^{- \\left(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}=3 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $e^{-\\ln t}=1/t$ because $t>0$.\n\nMultiply by the arc-length factor:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 3 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}3 t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=t^{2} + 1$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=2 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $2$ and $25$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 3 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{2}^{25}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{u}}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\left[u^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right]_{2}^{25} \\\\ &=\\left(125\\right)-\\left(2 \\sqrt{2}\\right) \\\\ &=125 - 2 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f7ec94c-f72e-5d3c-8407-9c385ad3844a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{364}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716690} {:answer/id #uuid "33477e53-4b88-52da-a2f5-0464be374084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{41 \\sqrt{82}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716692} {:answer/id #uuid "35c48841-ede4-595f-8e79-9f1e1bb31775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "840" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716694} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a53028-cb5f-5cfa-b712-ba666af0e44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{41 \\sqrt{82}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716706} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f193e9-7e35-5f74-aa9a-c183396e61ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{41 \\sqrt{82}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716707}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716706} :answer-field/id #uuid "1418ba9d-89a4-5d0b-921f-b42e9bb65550" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "da642773-ea13-5c29-a39c-4b681f02440b" :question/problem "![](images/55/5561c5106c9e0e95b7dbe6f7d0b646bae7236c36291332aa1f410bf2946e7d53.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C x y^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{1}{y}$ between $(1,1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3},3)$." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. The endpoint $y$-coordinates give $1\\le t\\le 3$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{t},t\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle - \\frac{1}{t^{2}},1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)^2+1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}}{t^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $t>0$, the denominator in the norm simplifies without changing sign.\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right) t^{6}=t^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nMultiply by the arc-length factor:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x y^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(t^{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}}{t^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}t^{3} \\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=t^{4} + 1$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=4 t^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $2$ and $82$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x y^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{2}^{82}\\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{6}\\right]_{2}^{82} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{41 \\sqrt{82}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{41 \\sqrt{82}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3035734-0085-5135-a2f2-18d62929903b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716973}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716973} :answer-field/id #uuid "7808619c-7ee0-5258-987d-7bf4842c0eee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716961} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3b64c25-85fb-5a34-9187-9cbd5fc054bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716976}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716976} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e67c8b4-fbe7-58ad-8477-3da57159a8c9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9addc0db-d1fd-5bd9-9804-688f43894ff4" :question/problem "![](images/50/503814b9764dae553ac77b9db5fd393ea93cf02a86989afc59551b1f83ee32a5.png)\n\nThe solid arc belongs to the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind the **closed interval** of $\\theta$ that traces exactly the solid arc once. Use endpoints between $0$ and $2\\pi$, including both endpoints of the arc.\n\n$\\theta\\in[${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse has center $(0,0)$, horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $6$. The standard parametrization travels counterclockwise from the rightmost point. Substituting the cardinal parameter values gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\\theta &(x,y)\\\\\\hline 0 & (3,0) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2} & (0,6) \\\\ \\pi & (-3,0) \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & (0,-6) \\\\ 2\\pi & (3,0)\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe **solid arc** is the lower-right part relative to the center. It starts at $(0,-6)$ when $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$ and ends at $(3,0)$ when $\\theta=2\\pi$. The counterclockwise traversal between these endpoints follows exactly the solid part, including both endpoints.\n\n![](images/4e/4e165eb4bd24aa6656234b1f4180e37ae090e929d1d56906d57e5735ab09c73e.png)\n\nHere $\\theta$ is the angle on the scaled unit circle $((x-h)/a,(y-k)/b)$; it is generally different from the geometric angle on the ellipse. The required restricted parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi}{2},2\\pi].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c15dff8a-a7d5-5b30-95f2-fe2adc99f1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716968}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716968} :answer-field/id #uuid "7712aed7-0353-5c5d-8121-9891dbfa354c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716960} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc1396e5-ab5b-5067-af06-01c67cd4c24a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716967}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716967} :answer-field/id #uuid "f88d8284-7937-53a9-92b8-5488c01eb50a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ce8a6bcf-e41c-5e7f-8e23-6720baf8c547" :question/problem "![](images/25/25239827db5874fa78a62a843d8775a2f619d9e73112bc28f9d5c9d3cda0792c.png)\n\nThe solid arc belongs to the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind the **closed interval** of $\\theta$ that traces exactly the solid arc once. Use endpoints between $0$ and $2\\pi$, including both endpoints of the arc.\n\n$\\theta\\in[${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse has center $(0,0)$, horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $1$. The standard parametrization travels counterclockwise from the rightmost point. Substituting the cardinal parameter values gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\\theta &(x,y)\\\\\\hline 0 & (2,0) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2} & (0,1) \\\\ \\pi & (-2,0) \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & (0,-1) \\\\ 2\\pi & (2,0)\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe **solid arc** is the upper-right part relative to the center. It starts at $(2,0)$ when $\\theta=0$ and ends at $(0,1)$ when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. The counterclockwise traversal between these endpoints follows exactly the solid part, including both endpoints.\n\n![](images/9c/9cc6d14cd425659b343c2e97ff9142f2a88e89862c494e9031bb2dbced4d6f25.png)\n\nHere $\\theta$ is the angle on the scaled unit circle $((x-h)/a,(y-k)/b)$; it is generally different from the geometric angle on the ellipse. The required restricted parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cbc3d5f-ee27-5f7d-9cab-2edbc0f32807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716962} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d8f0e9-0575-5328-a4cd-ca6e611ca268" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716957} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce3a1240-30cb-5b3a-97fb-ac985844f237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716969} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cdee286-db76-56ed-b49d-a21f481f1401" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d5444bbf-7006-55ec-9c8a-6b8f43564cdd" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcaeffe2f84a5dfd04c64c9947b1240b0275241c5ab5c3276aa778a830ecba54.png)\n\nThe solid arc belongs to the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=7\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind the **closed interval** of $\\theta$ that traces exactly the solid arc once. Use endpoints between $0$ and $2\\pi$, including both endpoints of the arc.\n\n$\\theta\\in[${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse has center $(0,0)$, horizontal radius $4$ and vertical radius $7$. The standard parametrization travels counterclockwise from the rightmost point. Substituting the cardinal parameter values gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\\theta &(x,y)\\\\\\hline 0 & (4,0) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2} & (0,7) \\\\ \\pi & (-4,0) \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & (0,-7) \\\\ 2\\pi & (4,0)\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe **solid arc** is the left part relative to the center. It starts at $(0,7)$ when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and ends at $(0,-7)$ when $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$. The counterclockwise traversal between these endpoints follows exactly the solid part, including both endpoints.\n\n![](images/a7/a7543597e30e12a24ed370c2cafeae050ad74706b5718c048ec43463521f46aa.png)\n\nHere $\\theta$ is the angle on the scaled unit circle $((x-h)/a,(y-k)/b)$; it is generally different from the geometric angle on the ellipse. The required restricted parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=7\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e165c6d7-86aa-571d-b86f-652fce6e3fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716975}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716975} :answer-field/id #uuid "10f814a2-fb93-5b77-bda4-c734e30b78a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716954} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfab0706-5b24-50ec-9f11-4c0e45f29d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716971} :answer-field/id #uuid "3275f1dc-c12c-5ae1-abf4-073feabad060" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f83b95b3-fdc7-58cd-9179-e6fdfb5c0756" :question/problem "![](images/97/9711e8b2a21b2888a077a0c12dde13de2719ce314c1379b1319e4363df529e16.png)\n\nThe solid arc belongs to the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind the **closed interval** of $\\theta$ that traces exactly the solid arc once. Use endpoints between $0$ and $2\\pi$, including both endpoints of the arc.\n\n$\\theta\\in[${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse has center $(0,0)$, horizontal radius $5$ and vertical radius $6$. The standard parametrization travels counterclockwise from the rightmost point. Substituting the cardinal parameter values gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\\theta &(x,y)\\\\\\hline 0 & (5,0) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2} & (0,6) \\\\ \\pi & (-5,0) \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & (0,-6) \\\\ 2\\pi & (5,0)\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe **solid arc** is the upper-left part relative to the center. It starts at $(0,6)$ when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and ends at $(-5,0)$ when $\\theta=\\pi$. The counterclockwise traversal between these endpoints follows exactly the solid part, including both endpoints.\n\n![](images/33/33bf8513f5b535844e3841f1f1c01a6ffd3718dda7cf0f609734d883306623e1.png)\n\nHere $\\theta$ is the angle on the scaled unit circle $((x-h)/a,(y-k)/b)$; it is generally different from the geometric angle on the ellipse. The required restricted parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1262723e-6804-5009-8bf0-48a0a093fdcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717188} :answer-field/id #uuid "923a020e-5c30-5206-ab40-8b04924508e1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717201} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "deaa0396-8565-5624-a279-0626a6233d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717210}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717210} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5a7a8e-dcaa-5347-b24b-41c8f8c41a30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "663a7a14-999d-5abd-8f40-02b09575681c" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5e14068c370ca692ffcaa52e375dc5fd3892108df5db98a95804ae8d63b9eb2.png)\n\nThe solid arc is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=3\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta$? Use $0\\leq\\theta\\leq2\\pi$ and include both filled endpoints.\n\n$\\theta\\in[$ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $]$" :question/worked-solution "The full circle has equations $x=h+r\\cos\\theta$, $y=k+r\\sin\\theta$, with $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$. Here the radius is $3$ and the center is $(0,0)$.\n\nThe parameter $\\theta$ measures the angle counterclockwise from the rightward horizontal ray through the center. The solid arc starts at $\\theta=\\pi$ and ends at $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, following increasing angle. Both endpoints are included.\n\n![](images/94/94853c2bca1ac50aaa4d6fe59d44c66b3a1da1909430b77d322ab34d3e747189.png)\n\nTherefore the required restriction is $\\theta\\in[\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}]$. The parametric equations of the arc are\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=3\\sin\\theta,\\qquad\\theta\\in[\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b909f7b5-c7a6-54e1-b2b6-16ea790f1956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717205} :answer-field/id #uuid "67635f0b-8204-524b-9949-d0d74f764791" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717195} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b3c9553-22b6-5c2f-bdf1-bf8725f2eaec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717187}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717187} :answer-field/id #uuid "7196e0b4-c4a4-5e4b-aa4d-85e1d7351068" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #