e both filled endpoints." :question/worked-solution "The full circle has equations $x=h+r\\cos\\theta$, $y=k+r\\sin\\theta$, with $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$. Here the radius is $5$ and the center is $(-3,-1)$.\n\nThe parameter $\\theta$ measures the angle counterclockwise from the rightward horizontal ray through the center. The solid arc starts at $\\theta=0$ and ends at $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, following increasing angle. Both endpoints are included.\n\n![](images/ab/ab123b01039725c01cc87fc8ac0100066f1c5dca61c47815c1436c3cf9315b64.png)\n\nTherefore the required restriction is $\\theta\\in[0,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}]$. The parametric equations of the arc are\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta-3,\\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta-1,\\qquad\\theta\\in[0,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}].\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "32b4b395-d621-5edb-9668-dfd7d4bb0a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\frac{\\pi}{2},2\\pi]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717240} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea21d42-437b-51d2-8501-01aec4a84539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\frac{\\pi}{2},2\\pi]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717242} {:answer/id #uuid "6bcc35f8-eb7e-521c-8f7a-d95501995d70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717243} {:answer/id #uuid "77846d30-8e0d-5f45-b4f9-65c105d93fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717244} {:answer/id #uuid "85889c85-eb87-5a99-98f6-e4dcd444fd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717245} {:answer/id #uuid "d52b8436-157a-5093-8338-b4de347e5460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717246} {:answer/id #uuid "f04672b8-bb22-5350-b6ce-22a7ef28d2b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11deac0e-b47c-5cfb-b7eb-fd9821c0c3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717304} {:answer/id #uuid "19afe672-b2de-5519-8eb5-a1d38da1872d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717305} {:answer/id #uuid "34300c4e-ee80-5f33-83ff-50943a4cb868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717308} {:answer/id #uuid "9fceecfc-5dda-5bbf-be78-15f77fc58af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717324} {:answer/id #uuid "b62a1f56-9c1a-5f0d-b2b0-66c57a8ed24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717328}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717328} :answer-field/id #uuid "48e6dc48-0bf8-534a-9d73-a8cd16619d55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3a96f7fd-f572-5d7a-9375-f9e5034997a0" :question/problem "![Tangent graph with marked geometric evidence.](images/2c/2cb91f0a2da33307bba275e36c49c6976925d35fabb9b00833f7ad2cb89fe5f7.png)\n\nThe graph shows $y=\\tan(Bx)+D$, where $B>0$ and $D$ are constants. The dashed lines are consecutive vertical asymptotes. What is $B+D$?" :question/worked-solution "The period is the distance between consecutive vertical asymptotes.\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nFor a tangent curve with $B>0$, $T=\\pi/B$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nB=\\frac{\\pi}{T}=\\frac{\\pi}{\\frac{2\\pi}{3}}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt $x=0$, $\\tan(0)=0$, so $y(0)=D$. The marked intercept gives $D=-4$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nB+D=\\frac{3}{2}+(-4)=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\tan(\\frac{3}{2}x)-4$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105bca85-7637-5f5c-8a02-208a01515d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717303} {:answer/id #uuid "2baa0878-7728-5fc7-ae8d-6798780ee5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717306} {:answer/id #uuid "31a586d0-64da-56e5-bb99-c81d6e3515cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717307} {:answer/id #uuid "7fae155e-cfc9-5ac5-9ebc-5063233f2911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717320} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3c6b23-f40d-55c2-9c4b-d18388d95a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717306} :answer-field/id #uuid "706ffd08-6fd6-5bc4-b443-8abe5000b6b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "79cebbb1-9c00-5e95-bd59-49b2e6645e10" :question/problem "![Tangent graph with marked geometric evidence.](images/14/144e49d33ac39863df9c18f00d32cde845ce9e4b99c57d54e1baac461545bc52.png)\n\nThe graph shows $y=\\tan(Bx)+D$, where $B>0$ and $D$ are constants. The dashed lines are consecutive vertical asymptotes. What is $D-B$?" :question/worked-solution "The period is the distance between consecutive vertical asymptotes.\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nFor a tangent curve with $B>0$, $T=\\pi/B$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nB=\\frac{\\pi}{T}=\\frac{\\pi}{\\frac{2\\pi}{3}}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt $x=0$, $\\tan(0)=0$, so $y(0)=D$. The marked intercept gives $D=-1$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nD-B=-1-\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\tan(\\frac{3}{2}x)-1$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d908afd-28e4-50cc-bab9-952e128c7653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717302} {:answer/id #uuid "71146265-7f1f-5632-bc55-911530ba4f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717318} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd9b1cb-e5ce-57e6-8fe8-fa14e1fe5d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717321} {:answer/id #uuid "98eed870-c812-5066-bfee-8a115cb3b484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717322} {:answer/id #uuid "d9aac647-69f8-5659-9f8f-7a6f1873749f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717333}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717333} :answer-field/id #uuid "afd9bb14-e969-5e76-99f9-11795374f96e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a9b51ec0-1aaf-5d97-8f89-b76dc1a78a02" :question/problem "![Tangent graph with marked geometric evidence.](images/5d/5db63ed3392aea9e48a7260a223e8dfb5c2e4cbc5d844e6e80a4ef40f258812c.png)\n\nThe graph shows $y=\\tan(Bx)+D$, where $B>0$ and $D$ are constants. The dashed lines are consecutive vertical asymptotes. What is $D-B$?" :question/worked-solution "The period is the distance between consecutive vertical asymptotes.\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi}{5}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{5}\\right)=\\frac{2\\pi}{5}.\n$$\n\nFor a tangent curve with $B>0$, $T=\\pi/B$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nB=\\frac{\\pi}{T}=\\frac{\\pi}{\\frac{2\\pi}{5}}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt $x=0$, $\\tan(0)=0$, so $y(0)=D$. The marked intercept gives $D=-4$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nD-B=-4-\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{13}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\tan(\\frac{5}{2}x)-4$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56fbe33c-fe4c-5573-b23b-07dfbccdefee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717314} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee0f9af-ce3b-5d11-a03d-ff6512930f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717316} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b8fbe1-87b3-5ddc-bc20-a018e25b70f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717325} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b331e0-ffda-5735-990c-13cbbd21ebfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717330} {:answer/id #uuid "d425d074-8325-56a4-9baf-c2d0327bee3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717331}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717316} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7fa1b24-fa82-5ac4-8022-47a91a35ef54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d6329bd5-d4d8-5e57-b45f-718b4f341e96" :question/problem "![Tangent graph with marked geometric evidence.](images/32/326b9969b577b22c820f76fd0a699fce3202b4b1f87c48f23677f8faceaafdc5.png)\n\nThe graph shows $y=\\tan(Bx)+D$, where $B>0$ and $D$ are constants. The dashed lines are consecutive vertical asymptotes. What is $B+D$?" :question/worked-solution "The period is the distance between consecutive vertical asymptotes.\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}-\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nFor a tangent curve with $B>0$, $T=\\pi/B$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nB=\\frac{\\pi}{T}=\\frac{\\pi}{\\frac{3\\pi}{2}}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nAt $x=0$, $\\tan(0)=0$, so $y(0)=D$. The marked intercept gives $D=4$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nB+D=\\frac{2}{3}+(4)=\\frac{14}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\tan(\\frac{2}{3}x)+4$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5985ca62-9f42-5898-9e08-f9f970a45d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717412} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8a3419-e83c-5baa-8ed3-dbae55b714b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717414} {:answer/id #uuid "6b622880-19e9-5197-bfeb-73b2fe120543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717415} {:answer/id #uuid "8fda5d67-25fc-553c-850e-fb0b9aabaea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717420} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fb613a-8413-5768-b5d5-9c494bccc35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717426}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717412} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d37d2d4-1f86-5174-8626-6279c2661ec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2bcc75cb-e013-5694-a9a7-8805494d449e" :question/problem "![Tangent graph with marked geometric evidence.](images/8a/8ac26e9fa71986dde7f27b570ee0f30393960d7288e916064a4750300a884e48.png)\n\nThe graph shows $y=\\tan(Bx)+D$, where $B>0$ and $D$ are constants. The y-axis and branch center are outside this cropped view. The dashed lines $x=\\pi$ and $x=3\\pi$ are consecutive vertical asymptotes. The marked point is $P=(\\frac{5\\pi}{2},5)$. What is $D-B$?" :question/worked-solution "The period is the distance between consecutive vertical asymptotes.\n\n$$\nT=3\\pi-(\\pi)=2\\pi.\n$$\n\nFor a tangent curve with $B>0$, $T=\\pi/B$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nB=\\frac{\\pi}{T}=\\frac{\\pi}{2\\pi}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe marked point is not a branch center. Substitute its coordinates to find the vertical translation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5&=\\tan\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{2}\\right)\\right)+D\\\\ & =\\tan\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right)+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTangent has period $\\pi$, so $\\tan\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right)=\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=1$. Hence\n\n$$\nD=5-(1)=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nD-B=4-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\tan(\\frac{1}{2}x)+4$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e71d660-71b3-5e41-b0f7-aebbf78f106d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717419} {:answer/id #uuid "c35548e3-1b51-5b83-9813-8ce59f7aa842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717428} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d0a52e-c3cb-5bb3-a69a-6ce2dac639e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717430} {:answer/id #uuid "e1465459-36e4-5351-8457-bd7e9abd036f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717433} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa92407-6c95-5f3d-9f7c-0e9d8380d02d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717434} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7a041a7-f94c-5651-ade1-9c52bac97f0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a0d62cf4-f815-58e3-9462-093554c0e2aa" :question/problem "![Tangent graph with marked geometric evidence.](images/8e/8e0672117a32ef15c4167369c1585a182be1f2ec0d82c107a867b9c5f45caf23.png)\n\nThe graph shows $y=\\tan(Bx)+D$, where $B>0$ and $D$ are constants. The y-axis and branch center are outside this cropped view. The dashed lines $x=-\\pi$ and $x=-\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ are consecutive vertical asymptotes. The marked point is $P=(-\\frac{5\\pi}{6},-2)$. What is $D-B$?" :question/worked-solution "The period is the distance between consecutive vertical asymptotes.\n\n$$\nT=-\\frac{\\pi}{3}-(-\\pi)=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nFor a tangent curve with $B>0$, $T=\\pi/B$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nB=\\frac{\\pi}{T}=\\frac{\\pi}{\\frac{2\\pi}{3}}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe marked point is not a branch center. Substitute its coordinates to find the vertical translation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2&=\\tan\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right)+D\\\\ & =\\tan\\left(-\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right)+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTangent has period $\\pi$, so $\\tan\\left(-\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right)=\\tan\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=-1$. Hence\n\n$$\nD=-2-(-1)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nD-B=-1-\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\tan(\\frac{3}{2}x)-1$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50f42592-ffce-5c72-b48c-0a0044b4fd67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717411} {:answer/id #uuid "aa127baa-03b6-567a-9424-d440862aef4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717424} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e5a48d-706d-5c6b-b56c-990d2cf8b54a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717425} {:answer/id #uuid "d32d745d-ed1d-5ad0-aea7-a99cf10ac6a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717429} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ffdcb5-d143-52f0-8799-6f0a1477132b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717424} :answer-field/id #uuid "de388c33-c3c4-583a-8871-3a50455b9180" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bf77f9dc-0d22-57a2-aff1-751379e95122" :question/problem "![Tangent graph with marked geometric evidence.](images/57/57631a29f2ca39823979ce4ddb6d183c8a9b3b987afffd37808a361bfbce535b.png)\n\nThe graph shows $y=\\tan(Bx)+D$, where $B>0$ and $D$ are constants. The y-axis and branch center are outside this cropped view. The dashed lines $x=-\\pi$ and $x=-\\frac{3\\pi}{5}$ are consecutive vertical asymptotes. The marked point is $P=(-\\frac{9\\pi}{10},0)$. What is $B+D$?" :question/worked-solution "The period is the distance between consecutive vertical asymptotes.\n\n$$\nT=-\\frac{3\\pi}{5}-(-\\pi)=\\frac{2\\pi}{5}.\n$$\n\nFor a tangent curve with $B>0$, $T=\\pi/B$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nB=\\frac{\\pi}{T}=\\frac{\\pi}{\\frac{2\\pi}{5}}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe marked point is not a branch center. Substitute its coordinates to find the vertical translation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0&=\\tan\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{9\\pi}{10}\\right)\\right)+D\\\\ & =\\tan\\left(-\\frac{9\\pi}{4}\\right)+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTangent has period $\\pi$, so $\\tan\\left(-\\frac{9\\pi}{4}\\right)=\\tan\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=-1$. Hence\n\n$$\nD=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nB+D=\\frac{5}{2}+(1)=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\tan(\\frac{5}{2}x)+1$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39282f56-a4a9-5ab6-91f0-91f68022ddc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717409} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7612b8-54f3-5297-9dc9-b4131b5c0359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{27}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717413} {:answer/id #uuid "7549a9ff-2238-51e9-a012-109399fe4238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717416} {:answer/id #uuid "890435e8-18b5-58e4-a524-4ca9396f53dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717417} {:answer/id #uuid "8e34ca44-84de-500d-baee-d8199454332d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717418}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717417} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e497718-2b1e-5f0e-b3d1-2974bd13074e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f41ed5fe-d56f-536c-8bfa-fbbb29818591" :question/problem "![Tangent graph with marked geometric evidence.](images/fd/fd0ac85839740c1009cfed7b493c2aa1b32721c916eb0a365d495c4630890a67.png)\n\nThe graph shows $y=\\tan(Bx)+D$, where $B>0$ and $D$ are constants. The y-axis and branch center are outside this cropped view. The dashed lines $x=-\\frac{6\\pi}{5}$ and $x=-\\frac{2\\pi}{5}$ are consecutive vertical asymptotes. The marked point is $P=(-\\frac{3\\pi}{5},-3)$. What is $B+D$?" :question/worked-solution "The period is the distance between consecutive vertical asymptotes.\n\n$$\nT=-\\frac{2\\pi}{5}-\\left(-\\frac{6\\pi}{5}\\right)=\\frac{4\\pi}{5}.\n$$\n\nFor a tangent curve with $B>0$, $T=\\pi/B$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nB=\\frac{\\pi}{T}=\\frac{\\pi}{\\frac{4\\pi}{5}}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe marked point is not a branch center. Substitute its coordinates to find the vertical translation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3&=\\tan\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi}{5}\\right)\\right)+D\\\\ & =\\tan\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTangent has period $\\pi$, so $\\tan\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=1$. Hence\n\n$$\nD=-3-(1)=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nB+D=\\frac{5}{4}+(-4)=-\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\tan(\\frac{5}{4}x)-4$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "446e5496-7d41-5464-bb2c-350ae0040723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717443} {:answer/id #uuid "854bb9ac-9e4b-53d1-ab3b-97598c4435d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717451} {:answer/id #uuid "da560f80-9465-510d-9d47-f21a874558c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717458} {:answer/id #uuid "f5de3a11-c48c-5ce9-baaa-e90f10c13963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717461} {:answer/id #uuid "fdeae8fc-9467-51b5-9597-5d031690d9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717458} :answer-field/id #uuid "29f42c1c-0d4d-5dec-a6cf-5bce72060841" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "082e4307-7463-5754-8adc-6e6aeda11497" :question/problem "![Tangent graph with marked geometric evidence.](images/50/5038519f379c15143ece73b6efd0c304883509fd9ca2496f6f8c22ea7e691ce0.png)\n\nThe graph shows $y=\\tan(Bx)+D$, where $B>0$ and $D$ are constants. The marked points $P$, $Q$, and $R$ are the centers of three consecutive branches. Their coordinates are $P=(-\\frac{\\pi}{4},-3)$, $Q=(0,-3)$, and $R=(\\frac{\\pi}{4},-3)$. What is $D-B$?" :question/worked-solution "The centers of consecutive branches are corresponding points one period apart. The span from $P$ to $R$ contains two periods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2T&=\\frac{\\pi}{4}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\\\ T&=\\frac{\\pi}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a tangent curve with $B>0$, $T=\\pi/B$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nB=\\frac{\\pi}{T}=\\frac{\\pi}{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}=4.\n$$\n\nAt $x=0$, $\\tan(0)=0$, so $y(0)=D$. The marked center $Q$ gives $D=-3$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nD-B=-3-(4)=-7.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\tan(4x)-3$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6561e6a1-1adb-57af-8b57-98f85e23cfaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717447} {:answer/id #uuid "7981e516-fcd0-50c1-b45b-4be42984dd34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717449} {:answer/id #uuid "7daabf62-3755-55de-9e51-d9831b9014bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717450} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa01275-a942-5a41-a6ae-715f0b61bfbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717455} {:answer/id #uuid "c92b5b31-d30d-5ff6-bdb7-7cf48bd8d6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717456}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717449} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ebee969-039c-5453-96a9-e8d30317637f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "29a54dbf-db7e-53eb-9a35-2820b71cf18e" :question/problem "![Tangent graph with marked geometric evidence.](images/f6/f6c85c983794b2e5c35357754a3305ce9ecff4b0f2366a3b02cb41a793130500.png)\n\nThe graph shows $y=\\tan(Bx)+D$, where $B>0$ and $D$ are constants. The marked points $P$, $Q$, and $R$ are the centers of three consecutive branches. Their coordinates are $P=(-\\frac{4\\pi}{3},2)$, $Q=(0,2)$, and $R=(\\frac{4\\pi}{3},2)$. What is $B+D$?" :question/worked-solution "The centers of consecutive branches are corresponding points one period apart. The span from $P$ to $R$ contains two periods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2T&=\\frac{4\\pi}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)=\\frac{8\\pi}{3}\\\\ T&=\\frac{4\\pi}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a tangent curve with $B>0$, $T=\\pi/B$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nB=\\frac{\\pi}{T}=\\frac{\\pi}{\\frac{4\\pi}{3}}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nAt $x=0$, $\\tan(0)=0$, so $y(0)=D$. The marked center $Q$ gives $D=2$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nB+D=\\frac{3}{4}+(2)=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\tan(\\frac{3}{4}x)+2$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f8d2be-4f4e-5c4b-bc4e-8db87a1453aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717438} {:answer/id #uuid "22da63d3-5ac9-586f-89c3-ae127c6316b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717439} {:answer/id #uuid "52b69de5-39a2-5da1-b041-a7d207d95f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717445} {:answer/id #uuid "890013a1-58f9-52ea-931a-62e60833dafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717452} {:answer/id #uuid "ea523d7c-a7f6-585b-9bcb-f000dbe3384d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717460}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717445} :answer-field/id #uuid "e521feee-8ed3-5809-bbbb-a82965d1e648" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "34709ae7-fbf0-5c43-8e6e-f3f373d7f584" :question/problem "![Tangent graph with marked geometric evidence.](images/9f/9fbb876c885f4dd10781f11b7fe15b0ace19f23ebc941d3619301bcca3fb78b6.png)\n\nThe graph shows $y=\\tan(Bx)+D$, where $B>0$ and $D$ are constants. The marked points $P$, $Q$, and $R$ are the centers of three consecutive branches. Their coordinates are $P=(-\\frac{2\\pi}{5},\\frac{1}{2})$, $Q=(0,\\frac{1}{2})$, and $R=(\\frac{2\\pi}{5},\\frac{1}{2})$. What is $D-B$?" :question/worked-solution "The centers of consecutive branches are corresponding points one period apart. The span from $P$ to $R$ contains two periods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2T&=\\frac{2\\pi}{5}-\\left(-\\frac{2\\pi}{5}\\right)=\\frac{4\\pi}{5}\\\\ T&=\\frac{2\\pi}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a tangent curve with $B>0$, $T=\\pi/B$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nB=\\frac{\\pi}{T}=\\frac{\\pi}{\\frac{2\\pi}{5}}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt $x=0$, $\\tan(0)=0$, so $y(0)=D$. The marked center $Q$ gives $D=\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nD-B=\\frac{1}{2}-\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=-2.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\tan(\\frac{5}{2}x)+\\frac{1}{2}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122a9772-a2bd-5e29-9008-bcb090200fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717437} {:answer/id #uuid "504316ee-e9ba-5638-8d6e-d22e7ceceb91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717444} {:answer/id #uuid "68b28ce6-7613-5448-958f-754f2577d15f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717448} {:answer/id #uuid "9abcc906-9905-5b45-bc6b-c5e1c4ffeb03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717453} {:answer/id #uuid "be642726-6609-58c5-9cbe-e5c80d0f44e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717454}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717454} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd1894da-ca2c-5e88-b1bf-84967fa5ccf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d08700db-e06a-56a5-aded-11268c15855e" :question/problem "![Tangent graph with marked geometric evidence.](images/66/6610324329cd6c5f0ff254f16351a993a3bdd25f9c5594ee09b1c8046a96629d.png)\n\nThe graph shows $y=\\tan(Bx)+D$, where $B>0$ and $D$ are constants. The marked points $P$, $Q$, and $R$ are the centers of three consecutive branches. Their coordinates are $P=(-\\frac{3\\pi}{4},3)$, $Q=(0,3)$, and $R=(\\frac{3\\pi}{4},3)$. What is $B+D$?" :question/worked-solution "The centers of consecutive branches are corresponding points one period apart. The span from $P$ to $R$ contains two periods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2T&=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}-\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\\\ T&=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a tangent curve with $B>0$, $T=\\pi/B$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nB=\\frac{\\pi}{T}=\\frac{\\pi}{\\frac{3\\pi}{4}}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nAt $x=0$, $\\tan(0)=0$, so $y(0)=D$. The marked center $Q$ gives $D=3$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nB+D=\\frac{4}{3}+(3)=\\frac{13}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\tan(\\frac{4}{3}x)+3$."} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9903bc-6b20-51a1-ac10-7b9ac3a5e852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)\\in[\\frac{8}{3},\\frac{10}{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717828} {:answer/id #uuid "d711f04a-c122-5341-bf16-88d212ebb6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)\\in[-\\frac{5}{4},-\\frac{3}{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717838} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e6f51e-c72d-5263-b935-95b0a4daf537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)\\in[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{9}{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717839} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ddc30f-fe36-5859-bc4f-01b83536e124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)\\in[-\\frac{11}{2},-\\frac{5}{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e671c06-778b-5af4-8cc3-45acc21065c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720255} {:answer/id #uuid "3c49b7e2-cf2e-5375-86f9-369cf9b8bfbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720257} {:answer/id #uuid "58f69f77-af14-5135-b5e0-c075011d8630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 - \\cos{\\left(1 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720267} {:answer/id #uuid "74241b8a-5d22-50da-9682-145985afb0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9 \\pi^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720271} {:answer/id #uuid "f98093e0-0a2b-51c3-85d7-07abe2f34312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\pi^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720282}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720255} :answer-field/id #uuid "42f4c21d-e8df-5ab5-ad92-d3ce083dd751" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "56d29d45-6d8a-5ecc-a2b5-b9af9ac06aa0" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, traversed in the direction of increasing $t$, where\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 3 \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)},\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(2 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Write $P=2 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 y$ and $Q=- x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x.$ A potential must satisfy $f_x=P$ and $f_y=Q$. Integrating the first component with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x y+g(y)\n$$\n\nMatch its $y$ derivative to the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x+g'(y)&=- x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x \\\\ g'(y)&=0 \\\\ g(y)&=0+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $K=0$, since a constant cancels in the endpoint difference. Thus\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x y\n$$\n\nDifferentiating verifies both components of the given field. For the stated orientation, the starting and ending points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A&=(0,0) \\\\ B&=(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the potential at each endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B)&=\\left(0\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}- 3 \\left(0\\right) \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=0 \\\\ f(A)&=\\left(0\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}- 3 \\left(0\\right) \\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(B)-f(A) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation. The speed is a square root of the sum of squared coordinate derivatives." :answer/id #uuid "3b38f325-46e1-59f4-9618-7c17eefaa973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{4}\\right)\\,\\left(1+\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720339} {:answer/feedback "The speed is a square root of the sum of squared coordinate derivatives." :answer/id #uuid "3c57b28f-dfb7-591f-8ae7-1461a71b9e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{2}\\right)\\,\\left(1+\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720340} {:answer/feedback "The parameter bounds, scalar substitution and positive arc-length factor all agree." :answer/id #uuid "75e1fd21-a7b0-54f6-914c-f70c08ccc592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{2}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720349} {:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation." :answer/id #uuid "95a87577-af60-5342-9b42-35b2039e699e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{3}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720356} {:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation. The speed is a square root of the sum of squared coordinate derivatives." :answer/id #uuid "d291feaa-0a6b-5640-970b-e27cb025c86f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{3}\\right)\\,\\left(1+\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720349} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a2aa7ac-41e7-5f91-86c4-31e917e6dfa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7cf692c7-198c-5ca9-a401-bf72fb6876a7" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ for $1\\le x\\le 3$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- 2 x^{3} y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Set $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\frac{1}{t}\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=- 2 t^{3} \\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 t^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{1}^{3} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{2}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation. The speed is a square root of the sum of squared coordinate derivatives. The parameter is $t=x$; horizontal levels are $y$-values, not parameter limits." :answer/id #uuid "15b32044-be8b-5d28-b3a6-300ac0efb837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}^{\\ln{\\left(11 \\right)}}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 4 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720372} {:answer/feedback "The parameter bounds, scalar substitution and positive arc-length factor all agree." :answer/id #uuid "82a1dcc7-a7f5-5f76-bb68-2851ea39e54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 4 t - 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720380} {:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation." :answer/id #uuid "890e1cd9-220f-5e62-9b99-6009fed5ddbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 4 t - \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720382} {:answer/feedback "The speed is a square root of the sum of squared coordinate derivatives." :answer/id #uuid "e4761705-4ad3-59ca-b9ff-9ed95cc9e067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 4 t - 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720396} {:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation. The speed is a square root of the sum of squared coordinate derivatives. The parameter is $t=x$; horizontal levels are $y$-values, not parameter limits." :answer/id #uuid "fbd17bd0-1eb9-5a9f-832d-92d472a3b0c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}^{\\ln{\\left(11 \\right)}}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 4 t - \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720397}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720380} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8cbaac3-1aff-5cf1-ba8e-831ddcd9dce3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2e3379fb-57b9-5bdb-bcd6-26c2c35cd8d3" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 2 \\right)}$ with $x>0$ between its intersections with the horizontal lines $y=\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}$ and $y=\\ln{\\left(11 \\right)}$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- 2 x e^{y} - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "First solve the intersection equations on the specified branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln(x^{2} + 2)=\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 2=3\\implies x=1\\\\\n\\ln(x^{2} + 2)=\\ln{\\left(11 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 2=11\\implies x=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=- 2 t e^{\\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)} - 2 \\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 t^{3} - 4 t - 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $e^{\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}}=t^{2} + 2$ because the logarithm argument is positive on the interval.\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{1}^{3} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 4 t - 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation. The speed is a square root of the sum of squared coordinate derivatives. The parameter is $t=x$; horizontal levels are $y$-values, not parameter limits." :answer/id #uuid "4b35e920-e00f-5258-b979-b190b3179588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}^{\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}\\left(t^{3} + t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 1}\\right)^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720375} {:answer/feedback "The parameter bounds, scalar substitution and positive arc-length factor all agree." :answer/id #uuid "70d31a18-2de3-5adb-92be-e458e14c4b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\left(t^{3} + t + \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 1}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720378} {:answer/feedback "The speed is a square root of the sum of squared coordinate derivatives." :answer/id #uuid "83795da5-ee88-5c1d-b1a3-eab7a1776511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\left(t^{3} + t + \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 1}\\right)^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720381} {:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation. The speed is a square root of the sum of squared coordinate derivatives. The parameter is $t=x$; horizontal levels are $y$-values, not parameter limits." :answer/id #uuid "a5ad2cf4-0fde-5705-b4c5-cd92dbb44df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}^{\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}\\left(t^{3} + t - \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 1}\\right)^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720386} {:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation." :answer/id #uuid "cf5ff3e9-0c0d-5899-9ba5-89407e0a4b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\left(t^{3} + t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 1}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720391}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720378} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fb23117-4026-50a3-aa5e-c9d5ac778484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8a8f5ae1-ca35-5385-acc3-098740e05b74" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}$ with $x<0$ between its intersections with the horizontal lines $y=\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}$ and $y=\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x e^{y} + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "First solve the intersection equations on the specified branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln(x^{2} + 1)=\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 1=5\\implies x=-2\\\\\n\\ln(x^{2} + 1)=\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 1=2\\implies x=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad -2\\le t\\le -1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 1}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 1}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=t e^{\\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)} + \\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=t^{3} + t + \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $e^{\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}}=t^{2} + 1$ because the logarithm argument is positive on the interval.\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-2}^{-1} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\left(t^{3} + t + \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 1}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation. Include the speed $\\sqrt{1+(y\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t))^2}$. The parameter is $t=x$; horizontal levels are $y$-values, not parameter limits." :answer/id #uuid "080c0fd2-ba17-51d9-b41f-8931293288ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}^{\\ln{\\left(18 \\right)}}\\left(2 t^{3} + 4 t + \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,1\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720371} {:answer/feedback "The parameter bounds, scalar substitution and positive arc-length factor all agree." :answer/id #uuid "4bcc8649-2d22-5d9b-a7d0-a786a33e2a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(2 t^{3} + 4 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720376} {:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation. Include the speed $\\sqrt{1+(y\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t))^2}$. The parameter is $t=x$; horizontal levels are $y$-values, not parameter limits." :answer/id #uuid "71498b74-8039-541c-a47b-5b461faf2d14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}^{\\ln{\\left(18 \\right)}}\\left(2 t^{3} + 4 t - 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,1\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720379} {:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation." :answer/id #uuid "afbf68f1-0773-5df3-9446-2106134aba11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(2 t^{3} + 4 t + \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720389} {:answer/feedback "Include the speed $\\sqrt{1+(y\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t))^2}$." :answer/id #uuid "e2a3e6c6-823d-53e4-bb9c-c135313d2fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(2 t^{3} + 4 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,1\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720395}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720376} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffef2526-1f50-57b1-a194-061110df31a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a5c265f6-66ae-518f-8377-83fba9dfedf4" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 2 \\right)}$ with $x>0$ between its intersections with the horizontal lines $y=\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}$ and $y=\\ln{\\left(18 \\right)}$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(2 x e^{y} + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "First solve the intersection equations on the specified branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln(x^{2} + 2)=\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 2=3\\implies x=1\\\\\n\\ln(x^{2} + 2)=\\ln{\\left(18 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 2=18\\implies x=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=2 t e^{\\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)} + 2 \\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=2 t^{3} + 4 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $e^{\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}}=t^{2} + 2$ because the logarithm argument is positive on the interval.\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{1}^{4} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(2 t^{3} + 4 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "Include the speed $\\sqrt{1+(y\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t))^2}$." :answer/id #uuid "2b0ef6b5-56a0-5999-b303-904b618b548d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(- t^{3} - 2 t + \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,1\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720373} {:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation. Include the speed $\\sqrt{1+(y\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t))^2}$. The parameter is $t=x$; horizontal levels are $y$-values, not parameter limits." :answer/id #uuid "919d5671-d6fb-5a37-b13e-c482ef37fb14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}^{\\ln{\\left(18 \\right)}}\\left(- t^{3} - 2 t - 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,1\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720384} {:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation." :answer/id #uuid "9216c699-d681-5c1a-897c-b269bb309190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(- t^{3} - 2 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720385} {:answer/feedback "The parameter bounds, scalar substitution and positive arc-length factor all agree." :answer/id #uuid "c74e979d-a881-5e7a-80c4-d16de66fec9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(- t^{3} - 2 t + \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720390} {:answer/feedback "Substitute both coordinates into the scalar function without dropping a factor or term or changing its operation. Include the speed $\\sqrt{1+(y\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t))^2}$. The parameter is $t=x$; horizontal levels are $y$-values, not parameter limits." :answer/id #uuid "d0051372-450b-50b3-9f63-3715fd0b0c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}^{\\ln{\\left(18 \\right)}}\\left(- t^{3} - 2 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,1\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720392}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720390} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5347cb4-356b-56c9-ab68-c3dfa7a0f621" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d6391a5d-141d-5543-a2c7-b930a2a3a808" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 2 \\right)}$ with $x>0$ between its intersections with the horizontal lines $y=\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}$ and $y=\\ln{\\left(18 \\right)}$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- x e^{y} + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "First solve the intersection equations on the specified branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln(x^{2} + 2)=\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 2=6\\implies x=2\\\\\n\\ln(x^{2} + 2)=\\ln{\\left(18 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 2=18\\implies x=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad 2\\le t\\le 4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=- t e^{\\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)} + \\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- t^{3} - 2 t + \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $e^{\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}}=t^{2} + 2$ because the logarithm argument is positive on the interval.\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{2}^{4} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(- t^{3} - 2 t + \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48a5ee9c-bc85-55bf-baa6-37c1d33df86c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721044} {:answer/id #uuid "a9adac7c-ee5c-5123-8b85-773f7ff7b51e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3} - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721056} {:answer/id #uuid "ad8afb78-868f-5923-b1f4-e63ee7f6f5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721059} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b57c84-ed48-5981-bab9-24669088405f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{64}{15} + \\frac{112 \\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721065} {:answer/id #uuid "e9bdc65e-2f27-51cb-b22a-9373e6802191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3} - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721056} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ac7a75-aadd-5016-8069-d6f771a1d38d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "63bcb904-6441-5f02-bed5-c91d698b73a1" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u,v^{2},\\frac{2 v^{3}}{3}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $1\\le v\\le \\sqrt{3}$." :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization over $D$, use $$\nA(S)=\\iint_D\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\|\\,\\mathrm dA\n$$\n\nDifferentiate and form the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 1,0,0\\rangle,\\\\\\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 0,2 v,2 v^{2}\\rangle,\\\\\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&0&0\\\\0&2 v&2 v^{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\langle 0,- 2 v^{2},2 v\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\|&=\\sqrt{4 v^{2} \\left(v^{2} + 1\\right)}\\\\&=2\\sqrt{v^2}\\sqrt{1+v^2}\\\\&=2 v \\sqrt{v^{2} + 1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v\\ge0$ throughout $D$, $\\sqrt{v^2}=v$. Integrate first with respect to $u$, then use $w=1+v^2$, $\\mathrm dw=2v\\,\\mathrm dv$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(S)&=\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\int_{0}^{2}2 v \\sqrt{v^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\&=2\\int_{2}^{4}\\sqrt w\\,\\mathrm dw\\\\&=2\\left[\\frac{2}{3}w^{3/2}\\right]_{2}^{4}\\\\&=\\frac{32}{3}-\\left(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)=\\frac{32}{3} - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26af3bdd-2436-56a3-bdae-43f4cc6bda7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721042} {:answer/id #uuid "8934ac83-a713-54ef-baaa-a2aee52964b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721051} {:answer/id #uuid "ab017909-c65f-5dc5-9e6b-85c2990ea820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2024}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721057} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c41c28-775b-59df-9dd1-7821987cacb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721061} {:answer/id #uuid "cc44f9af-bf05-5672-8a2d-257e0901bd09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{58}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721042} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe6b48ca-0385-5a15-a6bb-2ba6dceccabe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "de7c6657-4784-5a45-9fe8-6c47f8a84dcd" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u^{2},u v,\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $2\\le v\\le 4$." :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization over $D$, use $$\nA(S)=\\iint_D\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\|\\,\\mathrm dA\n$$\n\nDifferentiate and form the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 2 u,v,0\\rangle,\\\\\\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 0,u,v\\rangle,\\\\\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\2 u&v&0\\\\0&u&v\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\langle v^{2},- 2 u v,2 u^{2}\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\|&=\\sqrt{4 u^{4} + 4 u^{2} v^{2} + v^{4}}\\\\&=\\sqrt{(v^2+2u^2)^2}\\\\&=v^2+2u^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality holds because $v^2+2u^2\\ge0$. The parameter region is rectangular, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(S)&=\\int_{2}^{4}\\int_{0}^{1}(v^2+2u^2)\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\&=\\int_{2}^{4}\\left[uv^2+\\frac{2}{3}u^3\\right]_{0}^{1}\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\&=\\int_{2}^{4}(v^{2} + \\frac{2}{3})\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\&=\\left[\\frac{v \\left(v^{2} + 2\\right)}{3}\\right]_{2}^{4}\\\\&=(24)-(4)=20.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49998140-c020-50f3-bc46-760346914c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7060}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721045} {:answer/id #uuid "7b79def2-73b2-56e6-a6d1-c0ac6a0ad4b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721050} {:answer/id #uuid "c559efee-0afa-5e07-99e5-18e0c71030fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721063} {:answer/id #uuid "db9196b3-22fb-5ab8-93e9-09b5b6662949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{104}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721066} {:answer/id #uuid "e0950f35-8a5b-5cf0-9e5a-a9d950c5bfe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721069}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721063} :answer-field/id #uuid "0916df20-b8a0-55e2-ab6f-48f34861f04a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e1872319-de7d-549c-b2c1-146d774d69e8" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u^{2},u v,\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFor $1\\le u\\le 3$ and $1\\le v\\le 3$." :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization over $D$, use $$\nA(S)=\\iint_D\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\|\\,\\mathrm dA\n$$\n\nDifferentiate and form the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 2 u,v,0\\rangle,\\\\\\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 0,u,v\\rangle,\\\\\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\2 u&v&0\\\\0&u&v\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\langle v^{2},- 2 u v,2 u^{2}\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\|&=\\sqrt{4 u^{4} + 4 u^{2} v^{2} + v^{4}}\\\\&=\\sqrt{(v^2+2u^2)^2}\\\\&=v^2+2u^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality holds because $v^2+2u^2\\ge0$. The parameter region is rectangular, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(S)&=\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}(v^2+2u^2)\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\&=\\int_{1}^{3}\\left[uv^2+\\frac{2}{3}u^3\\right]_{1}^{3}\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\&=\\int_{1}^{3}(2 v^{2} + \\frac{52}{3})\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\&=\\left[\\frac{2 v \\left(v^{2} + 26\\right)}{3}\\right]_{1}^{3}\\\\&=(70)-(18)=52.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18559774-ee7f-5b2e-bff1-96c52822c7b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8 - \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721078} {:answer/id #uuid "7caf86b7-61ab-5be3-a17b-793325b05549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 + \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721091} {:answer/id #uuid "cf766c8e-41e5-560f-a6c8-6c9260687021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 - \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721100} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b79026-6763-5dfb-bd2e-db147736af83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4 + \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721101} {:answer/id #uuid "f3cd1e8c-8d0e-559c-b79f-7ecac71fa511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721091} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5b882e1-8c5a-56a4-8f0f-be9a737fae6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79a03524-42ec-54a2-874e-ec327283e7ac" :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F=\\left(- 2 y^{2} \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ at the point $(- \\frac{\\pi}{2},1)$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, use $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F=P_x+Q_y$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_x&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- 2 y^{2} \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 4 y^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\\\\nQ_y&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{3 x}{y}\\\\\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F&=P_x+Q_y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe point satisfies the domain condition $y>0$.\n\nEvaluate after differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F)(- \\frac{\\pi}{2},1)&=4+\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=4 + \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f25da19-6ace-5aa9-8701-abc5cd80779c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 5 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721077} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc7172e-2edb-578f-aa82-a8f57841ab90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721089} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1caacc-c4c6-5994-ba14-589bdcb722b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721092} {:answer/id #uuid "e162184e-1d5e-5a8f-9d02-bd4846feebbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 4 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721103} {:answer/id #uuid "fa56ac6a-8906-57dc-a680-150c7d005dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721110}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721103} :answer-field/id #uuid "778dd489-8d57-58cd-86d5-c155cd8d69fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac219544-4f33-505b-b05b-2cc0ccf1e5a5" :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F=\\left(e^{y} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- 3 e^{y} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi}{2},1)$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, use $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F=P_x+Q_y$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_x&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(e^{y} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- e^{y} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\\\\nQ_y&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- 3 e^{y} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 3 e^{y} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\\\\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F&=P_x+Q_y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate after differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F)(\\frac{\\pi}{2},1)&=- e+\\left(- 3 e\\right)\\\\\n&=- 4 e.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "41055ac7-fdbf-575b-82f1-e2a834613816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721859} {:answer/id #uuid "4a44376e-0c3c-5d37-8fd2-b713f1dd28ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721860} {:answer/id #uuid "84fa57fb-a948-57c6-8aef-39837b08f817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721864} {:answer/id #uuid "9a98a2fa-80e0-54f2-bf88-bcd68752aa22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721867} {:answer/id #uuid "9acd9fd0-afd5-546e-9f86-ab37967a2f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721868} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a3dc78-9dd7-5f3a-af35-64042bf1a3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721872} {:answer/id #uuid "052d5f34-1e0e-5282-b101-978370b35877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721902} {:answer/id #uuid "4a56347d-f156-54d8-becf-9401900b9460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721907} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd1942f-17d9-5262-8107-8b248abb2d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721910} {:answer/id #uuid "b35421ac-4249-5bfd-9205-469cc554fe25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721916} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d9a5e6-582b-57e3-9d26-f4754f595458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721917} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a9bd3d-1dfb-541d-8c7e-97084768fc31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721919} {:answer/id #uuid "354f4645-ce5d-5125-a619-6f7af51ff5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18dc8ed5-0eb6-5265-8f96-706d4de24a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721921} {:answer/id #uuid "80836a02-9e1d-58a7-95b1-6e0ac8e8eccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721929} {:answer/id #uuid "80f26a90-5e26-5e52-b24f-84f92d44444e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721930} {:answer/id #uuid "c930c7cd-1e21-5052-8582-78a62393007a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721935} {:answer/id #uuid "cd08f9a5-6b96-599b-9fc7-16b95f50d978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721936}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721930} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0a7816e-e285-5174-afab-861180b3d7f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4262c717-975c-5907-a4c0-2f112dda35e8" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$, where $r>0$, and Cartesian coordinates $(2,0)$. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{\\pi}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nUse $x=R\\cos\\phi$ and $y=R\\sin\\phi$ for a point with polar coordinates $(R,\\phi)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $A=(2,0)$ lies on the positive $x$-axis, its distance from the origin is $r=2$. Thus the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2r,\\tfrac{\\pi}{3})=(4,\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe lesson's right triangle shows why the Cartesian coordinates of a point are its radius times the cosine and sine of its angle:\n\n![](images/20/205b3684042b0ab0261a4d56800cd89a10a7816f8022befcf6c664337f834380.png)\n\nUsing the radius $4$ and angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ of $B$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=4\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=4\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}=2,\\\\\ny_B&=4\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=4\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(2,2\\sqrt{3})$."} {:answer/id #uuid "5c704991-320f-5bda-9cb5-02e76d853f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721926} {:answer/id #uuid "6784e06f-23df-53f4-b0e4-8b398e8d6d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721927} {:answer/id #uuid "93fbe734-9a08-5bd2-b375-ffb58a8eb0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721932} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9d4691-e5d1-544f-b3ee-943ba315c03e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354f4645-ce5d-5125-a619-6f7af51ff5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721922} {:answer/id #uuid "5c704991-320f-5bda-9cb5-02e76d853f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721926} {:answer/id #uuid "6784e06f-23df-53f4-b0e4-8b398e8d6d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721927} {:answer/id #uuid "93fbe734-9a08-5bd2-b375-ffb58a8eb0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721932} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9d4691-e5d1-544f-b3ee-943ba315c03e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721934}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721927} :answer-field/id #uuid "36058a43-ce58-5e65-b557-e8894698601b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "eb1691f4-2c26-5ec5-bcce-bef44dcb6e7a" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta)$ and Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{3},1)$. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(3,\\theta+\\frac{\\pi}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$." :question/worked-solution "First, find the reference angle of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\alpha=\\arctan\\left(\\left|\\frac{y_A}{x_A}\\right|\\right)=\\arctan\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nSince $x_A<0$ and $y_A>0$, the point $A$ lies in the second quadrant. We may take\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\pi-\\alpha=\\frac{5\\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nThe angle of $B$ is therefore\n\n$$\n\\phi_B=\\theta+\\frac{\\pi}{3}=\\frac{5\\pi}{6}+\\frac{2\\pi}{6}=\\frac{7\\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nAny coterminal choice of $\\theta$ gives the same Cartesian point. The polar coordinates of $B$ are $(3,\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. Now convert using $x_B=3\\cos\\phi_B$ and $y_B=3\\sin\\phi_B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=3\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=3\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\\\\ny_B&=3\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=3\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e0b4c99-02e6-5ee8-9770-deb5fbe3546a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721931} :answer-field/id #uuid "721c0c4a-7e23-5976-ac01-3b51e61bc51c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721928} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4760f36b-168c-543c-b09e-b2f8a65c3a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721925}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721925} :answer-field/id #uuid "aadb8ad8-c463-55cc-b83a-d92fdc80bb3f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f84b9c04-ea6a-54fe-bfad-5eced44c6532" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi}{4})$, where $r>0$, and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{3},\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "First, recover the radius of $A$ from its Cartesian coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr&=\\sqrt{x_A^2+y_A^2}\\\\\n &=\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^2+(\\sqrt{2})^2}=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. We use $x_B=R_B\\cos\\phi_B$ and $y_B=R_B\\sin\\phi_B$ with the radius and angle of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\\\\ny_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{1}{3})$. Both coordinates are negative, as expected for an angle in the third quadrant."} {:answer/id #uuid "b4cc1548-7364-579c-b0d5-7317dfbd4e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac12,\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722053 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e91e45d-c21c-5ca1-abec-1a6b81c8f0ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722063} {:answer/id #uuid "861d5e5d-b164-56de-870e-32986f2d831a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722068} {:answer/id #uuid "97395662-b225-54e6-b185-5e70ecb651ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722070} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4bb0f8-93c1-5c17-a06d-6f62749718d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722079} {:answer/id #uuid "f34163e6-10a3-5f14-adaa-3316e745a929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722085}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722068} :answer-field/id #uuid "bce8a310-ffcb-5c16-a83c-8fff1197e59b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1312b275-cc74-5b75-8dea-a645bdff1d43" :question/problem "![](images/14/140df7664edfa54f0af250561876700abdc3fec016b916cdf463925660a3806b.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x + \\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=\\left(3,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ to $B=\\left(2,0\\right)$, as shown." :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the requested integral. The path can be parameterized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 - t,\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{t}{2}\\rangle.\n$$\nThe parameter runs from $t=0$ at $A$ to $t=1$ at $B$. Use the signed coordinate derivative $y'(t)=- \\frac{1}{2}$.\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=- t + \\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\right)} + 3\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- t + \\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\right)} + 3\\right)\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{2} - \\frac{\\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor the trigonometric term, the argument derivative is $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\right)=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Keep that argument when applying the sine or cosine primitive.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{2} - \\frac{\\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{3 t}{2} + \\sin{\\left(\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{5}{4}\\right)-\\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{9}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n The differential is $\\mathrm{d}y$, so an arc-length speed factor is not used."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06af3c32-e74f-51ab-b11a-5ddf6422c314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} 16 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722151} {:answer/id #uuid "22b1434a-9a3d-5018-95c4-f00e6c84ef79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722155} {:answer/id #uuid "397278ed-a1cd-5aac-b506-315c7fcc1592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} - 16 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722160} {:answer/id #uuid "6076489c-3aed-50aa-8e20-75b23058283d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{12}} - 64 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722165} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a0e414-cc96-5776-8ed0-1cde24490ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} - \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722176}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722160} :answer-field/id #uuid "09bf36a8-ca9e-5c8e-8f89-85b9622c98a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4917643a-843b-51be-920e-1a9345c227fd" :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/3d/3da9ed3b002a954b70f4aae8a7ee862682c8379688863b87dd49dea16e4eb905.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $16 x^{2} + y^{2}=16$, traversed clockwise through one quarter of the ellipse from $(\\frac{1}{2},- 2 \\sqrt{3})$ to $(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-2)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated clockwise direction. At the initial endpoint, $\\cos t=\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin t=\\frac{y}{-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. Thus the initial parameter in $[0,2\\pi)$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Continue the parameter through $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to follow the stated arc. The parameter starts at $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ and ends at $\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\le t\\le \\frac{5 \\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^{2}\\\\&=16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 16 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} - 16 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54558b04-4dae-59ef-8864-9f6997f028e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} 12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722163} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4de76b-811c-5743-a96b-909a5caef99f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} 36 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722164} {:answer/id #uuid "63951fe2-6c6a-5111-976b-67bcfc64d9cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} 54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722166} {:answer/id #uuid "8357452d-bae1-57a7-9436-a36ac84cbfd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} - 36 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722171} {:answer/id #uuid "8b1cf199-a441-5a32-bbde-738cd19a683c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} - 54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722171} :answer-field/id #uuid "331f788d-ec63-5044-8a01-d726b48fbe41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "67286858-7abb-5ba2-873d-af5fc384dec1" :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/91/91c0bb3ccbb239ce4a24ad76245345da38cd29e495475bd8d306132af414b1eb.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $4 x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=36$, traversed counterclockwise through one quarter of the ellipse from $(- \\frac{3}{2},\\sqrt{3})$ to $(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},-1)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated counterclockwise direction. At the initial endpoint, $\\cos t=\\frac{x}{3}=- \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin t=\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. Thus the initial parameter in $[0,2\\pi)$ is $\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}$. Continue the parameter through $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to follow the stated arc. The parameter starts at $\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}$ and ends at $\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\le t\\le \\frac{7 \\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^{2}\\\\&=12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 36 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} - 36 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722169 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "294fda8a-0188-5465-866d-1334c8a39ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} - 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722156} {:answer/id #uuid "342f3c6e-d1cb-5506-8daf-a73a07e2fc3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} - 54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722159} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad170ca-7d6c-52fb-b9a0-44ef87d57987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} 36 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722168} {:answer/id #uuid "b2984a31-8381-50b4-ac65-d1d255dcfa47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} - 36 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722174} {:answer/id #uuid "f32651c4-ea75-52cd-a9d2-d2d86dd23648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} 54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722177} :answer-field/id #uuid "f33ddf97-22b8-5880-a212-0cfffaaada70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6eec3085-13bc-5a40-8a92-15a08014c3ed" :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/31/310cd83111af3ba8b0cb57f3ab0268b69eff07244a22f36116ecde25faf73877.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $4 x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=36$, traversed clockwise through one quarter of the ellipse from $(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},-1)$ to $(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\sqrt{3})$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated clockwise direction. At the initial endpoint, $\\cos t=\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\sin t=\\frac{y}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}$. Thus the initial parameter in $[0,2\\pi)$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$. Continue the parameter through $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to follow the stated arc. The parameter starts at $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and ends at $\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le t\\le \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^{2}(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(- 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)})(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} 54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "170f42d6-b20c-59a8-b5cb-b3fe743fefc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722153} {:answer/id #uuid "1eebcbee-88d2-5288-96f2-0cbcdd6d4b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} 8 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722154} {:answer/id #uuid "33542dff-a651-5de3-9286-e3c052358206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} - 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722158} {:answer/id #uuid "846e7265-3799-5604-afcf-93188c391f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{12}} 8 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722172} {:answer/id #uuid "c063e007-4942-56c1-b38a-89d783e6ed2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} - 4 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722175} :answer-field/id #uuid "4475143e-12ce-5b67-8f71-86c7782ceafb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7945137f-f35c-59c8-82e6-c0a5808f9745" :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/b5/b5ab08b1bd65b8c691bcbb7e59820f20ff9b6e226d8590efe10c6656e8dab9d5.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=4$, traversed clockwise through one quarter of the ellipse from $(1,- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ to $(- \\sqrt{3},- \\frac{1}{2})$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{1}=1$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated clockwise direction. At the initial endpoint, $\\cos t=\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin t=\\frac{y}{-1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. Thus the initial parameter in $[0,2\\pi)$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Continue the parameter through $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to follow the stated arc. The parameter starts at $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ and ends at $\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\le t\\le \\frac{5 \\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^{2}\\\\&=2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 4 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} - 4 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7cd0e27-36a8-5d99-a9fb-36ec474df23a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722269} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca08921-b3b0-58b7-b2a9-3ad78255f07c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722265} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88498f4b-1173-579b-b9b9-1b34756b6f45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722262} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2d987e8-1a14-5576-a516-dc2dc166699f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "786a4c08-1e4b-5744-a7df-59615a923660" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\theta)$ and Cartesian coordinates $(0,-\\sqrt{2})$. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta-\\frac{\\pi}{4})$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "The point $A$ lies on the negative $y$-axis, so we may take $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$.\n\nApply the signed angular offset:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\phi_B&=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(2,\\frac{5\\pi}{4})$. Convert each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=2\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right)=2\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-\\sqrt{2} \\\\\ny_B&=2\\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right)=2\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc6f1a58-5a5c-5f21-ae1c-32200fa5f430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722270}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722270} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c324edd-be6e-5ba1-bb77-585ca097ea4e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722253} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7b67aa0-719c-56dc-9707-8274cf1a1f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722273}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722273} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeb71845-bf4f-564d-9590-ec8c8721d214" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c1240a8-8c2d-5714-90d4-591fbd172f14" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{5\\pi}{3})$ and Cartesian coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{6})$. The radius $r$ is positive. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "First, recover the positive radius of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr&=\\sqrt{x_A^2+y_A^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^2+(-\\sqrt{6})^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the radial relationship:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_B&=2\\cdot(2\\sqrt{2})=4\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. Convert each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=4\\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=4\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-2\\sqrt{6} \\\\\ny_B&=4\\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=4\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-2\\sqrt{6},-2\\sqrt{2})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e19af3f0-3dda-5c68-bcaa-a3517e941d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722271}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722271} :answer-field/id #uuid "08e0815f-88c3-52c6-9127-868390879814" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722249} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67c7f912-4e88-5ca8-8684-c08c2e9cdff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722256}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722256} :answer-field/id #uuid "894f7550-df6f-55a2-aae7-b6a13d42f378" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "90ee7654-bfe0-5ac6-8d2d-9babd3746dce" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,\\theta)$ and Cartesian coordinates $(0,6)$. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(3,\\theta+\\frac{\\pi}{4})$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "The point $A$ lies on the positive $y$-axis, so we may take $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nApply the signed angular offset:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\phi_B&=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(3,\\frac{3\\pi}{4})$. Convert each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=3\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=3\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\ny_B&=3\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=3\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "332adade-bed7-5d79-9394-ec3c2b13fa76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722252} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ce1a4df-0e6f-536f-91d3-6a3052462204" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722254} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707a795f-2229-58f3-9dc5-347fd74b061a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722258}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722258} :answer-field/id #uuid "6db39753-96e1-5610-89f0-fbd0fad54a19" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b0d248b9-2238-5a27-85b7-408b469ae92e" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{4\\pi}{3})$ and Cartesian coordinates $(-3,-3\\sqrt{3})$. The radius $r$ is positive. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(r^{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{4})$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "First, recover the positive radius of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr&=\\sqrt{x_A^2+y_A^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+(-3\\sqrt{3})^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{36}=6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the radial relationship:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_B&=(6)^2=36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(36,\\frac{3\\pi}{4})$. Convert each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=36\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=36\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-18\\sqrt{2} \\\\\ny_B&=36\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=36\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=18\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-18\\sqrt{2},18\\sqrt{2})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83880857-9538-54b4-aede-58b1f1e4a758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722261} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a6283db-5ae5-5c6c-b601-810953907a31" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722251} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15319a44-8550-5918-9074-d06efaff0ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722250}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722250} :answer-field/id #uuid "5446c58b-eb64-5ab9-8cc0-f15a69b691a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b4b11be6-bf1b-5525-9fda-436f7f9aa080" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{4\\pi}{3})$ and Cartesian coordinates $(-1,-\\sqrt{3})$. The radius $r$ is positive. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{3},\\pi)$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "First, recover the positive radius of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr&=\\sqrt{x_A^2+y_A^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+(-\\sqrt{3})^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{4}=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the radial relationship:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_B&=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot(2)=\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\pi)$. Convert each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\cos\\left(\\pi\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(-1\\right)=-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\\ny_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\sin\\left(\\pi\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(0\\right)=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{2}{3},0)$."} {:answer/id #uuid "025b4cd8-cb52-5559-a7a7-9afb3a0cae1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722275} {:answer/id #uuid "108bbd60-821c-5efb-bc28-2da38c62ed16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722276} {:answer/id #uuid "15fd0b07-8629-528b-acf2-4c453cb45d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722278} {:answer/id #uuid "197a271a-b76c-5504-86e7-3f49d0398436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722280} {:answer/id #uuid "36e69d7f-3e32-5a28-b2aa-813e391af69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{3}{4},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "197a271a-b76c-5504-86e7-3f49d0398436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722280} {:answer/id #uuid "36e69d7f-3e32-5a28-b2aa-813e391af69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{3}{4},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722282} {:answer/id #uuid "8cce678e-c102-5b76-829c-9fa8050021bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{1}{2},- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722296} {:answer/id #uuid "a794e539-ea61-522c-a388-dfac8d653207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722298} {:answer/id #uuid "a99aa229-1756-5c45-b565-b200e0d3ce39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,- 3 \\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722299}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722280} :answer-field/id #uuid "319d266e-3dd3-5af7-91ea-e7ddec41a8d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "437a97e2-4575-5c68-bc87-7a2927b210d7" :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $3$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=3$ and $\\theta=\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}$. The angle is in quadrant $3$ with reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Thus $\\cos\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$. Calculate the horizontal coordinate first, then the vertical coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta\\\\\n&=(3)\\cos\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\\n&=(3)\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=r\\sin\\theta\\\\\n&=(3)\\sin\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\\n&=(3)\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian coordinates are $(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2})$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{4}=9=r^2$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d2b7729-5417-5844-90bf-2d11c0239c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722288} :answer-field/id #uuid "54545cd3-2159-5429-bbf7-56b03db3f679" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722290} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "437d6b6a-33df-55f3-8965-90ab771044fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722285}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722285} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6feb54c-d46c-55ff-88fe-fd025c5bceb7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "448b16cb-5709-5e8b-a344-76b65d4955c7" :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $3$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$.\n\n$(x,y)=(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=3$ and $\\theta=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$. The angle is in quadrant $2$ with reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$. Thus $\\cos\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$. Calculate the horizontal coordinate first, then the vertical coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta\\\\\n&=(3)\\cos\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)\\\\\n&=(3)\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=r\\sin\\theta\\\\\n&=(3)\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)\\\\\n&=(3)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian coordinates are $(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2})$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}=9=r^2$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025b4cd8-cb52-5559-a7a7-9afb3a0cae1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722275} {:answer/id #uuid "108bbd60-821c-5efb-bc28-2da38c62ed16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722276} {:answer/id #uuid "8b832af9-27f7-5eec-912c-46fcc8bfbe4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722295} {:answer/id #uuid "952e7f5c-f55b-59f0-af2d-97a11c4fcd0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{16},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722297} {:answer/id #uuid "abddd75b-9c7f-5d38-ad0c-f927f3918abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722300}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722295} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f2b1955-808b-5387-9b70-8fa901803b62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4641e0f6-47b8-546f-adac-8949e16b137b" :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $\\frac{1}{4}$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $225^{\\circ}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\theta=225^{\\circ}$. The angle is in quadrant $3$ with reference angle $45^{\\circ}$. Thus $\\cos(225^{\\circ})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $\\sin(225^{\\circ})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$. Calculate the horizontal coordinate first, then the vertical coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cos(225^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=r\\sin\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\sin(225^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian coordinates are $(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8})$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=\\frac{1}{32}+\\frac{1}{32}=\\frac{1}{16}=r^2$."} {:answer/id #uuid "52572a14-ad04-5725-8744-d5f87a2144d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722289} {:answer/id #uuid "55e14521-0880-504f-93b7-2a92e43d6038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8},\\frac{1}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722291} {:answer/id #uuid "8b832af9-27f7-5eec-912c-46fcc8bfbe4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722295} {:answer/id #uuid "8cce678e-c102-5b76-829c-9fa8050021bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{1}{2},- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722296} {:answer/id #uuid "952e7f5c-f55b-59f0-af2d-97a11c4fcd0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{16},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722297} {:answer/id #uuid "a794e539-ea61-522c-a388-dfac8d653207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722298} {:answer/id #uuid "abddd75b-9c7f-5d38-ad0c-f927f3918abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722300} {:answer/id #uuid "dce31149-a8d9-5a49-b746-1a5d85e9d298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},- \\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15fd0b07-8629-528b-acf2-4c453cb45d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722278} {:answer/id #uuid "52572a14-ad04-5725-8744-d5f87a2144d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722289} {:answer/id #uuid "55e14521-0880-504f-93b7-2a92e43d6038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8},\\frac{1}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722291} {:answer/id #uuid "72030818-9dab-53fa-8f4f-fe9e554b6b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\sqrt{3},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722292} {:answer/id #uuid "dce31149-a8d9-5a49-b746-1a5d85e9d298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},- \\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722303}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722289} :answer-field/id #uuid "74640ca2-eb0a-5c84-9a7c-a6821e5b3b9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "faa5d0c6-91cf-5871-b4c9-00908ab21c68" :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $\\frac{1}{2}$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $150^{\\circ}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\theta=150^{\\circ}$. The angle is in quadrant $2$ with reference angle $30^{\\circ}$. Thus $\\cos(150^{\\circ})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\sin(150^{\\circ})=\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$. Calculate the horizontal coordinate first, then the vertical coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cos(150^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=r\\sin\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\sin(150^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian coordinates are $(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{1}{4})$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=\\frac{3}{16}+\\frac{1}{16}=\\frac{1}{4}=r^2$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "549b9faf-456f-5427-8e4e-574fc083dc8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722399}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722399} :answer-field/id #uuid "20301d49-58a8-5cd0-a56a-d38006547e58" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ed64302-5f3f-576f-a7fc-6f27b594141b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722394}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722394} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ec4df2e-aaf9-5f25-a801-a1d7c5272895" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0189301b-08d7-5d9c-92d1-67224f073476" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b17d9c57364b57f5a41d1ef1eba46aefb14501d4e8e6ced0fc976bc1b6075c7c.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,\\pi)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}$.\n\nDetermine the exact Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $6$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is counterclockwise; add the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=\\pi+\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}=\\frac{6\\pi}{6}+\\frac{5\\pi}{6}=\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(6,\\frac{11 \\pi}{6})$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=6\\cos\\left(\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}\\right)=6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=3 \\sqrt{3},\\\\y &=6\\sin\\left(\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}\\right)=6\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(3 \\sqrt{3},-3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97fd5a83-2226-53ba-8676-0f2031217c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722408}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722408} :answer-field/id #uuid "18e416d7-df20-52e5-9c17-7fef7564e7d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722395} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "284a47e3-a03d-594e-ac9e-4c1be102a466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722397}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722397} :answer-field/id #uuid "59332158-366d-514f-8e32-fc08973153eb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5d590ec8-8697-55da-a041-c23ad60e4df7" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1c9a038c2ca74c36cb49b3677b4ba6b80e4ed7fc7d0899eb828a4d6ecbace2e0.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(3 \\sqrt{2},\\frac{5 \\pi}{6})$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=\\frac{7 \\pi}{12}$.\n\nDetermine the exact Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $3 \\sqrt{2}$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is clockwise; subtract the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}-\\frac{7 \\pi}{12}=\\frac{10\\pi}{12}-\\frac{7\\pi}{12}=\\frac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(3 \\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi}{4})$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=3 \\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=3 \\sqrt{2}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=3,\\\\y &=3 \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=3 \\sqrt{2}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(3,3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da8c46f7-a35e-561e-ab33-f2475589143c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722413}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722413} :answer-field/id #uuid "a05ad7d4-1093-5275-bbae-e3eea8b478ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722411} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079f93d1-76c4-5bda-89fd-b448b1abf5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722393}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722393} :answer-field/id #uuid "e63c7f79-30ae-52ea-ae25-2efcecfe68df" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "92556bc8-7ad9-546a-896c-0a996e5fdbb3" :question/problem "!