nterclockwise; add the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=225^\\circ+75^\\circ=300^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(9,300^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=9\\cos\\left(300^\\circ\\right)=9\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{9}{2},\\\\y &=9\\sin\\left(300^\\circ\\right)=9\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=- \\frac{9 \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{9}{2},- \\frac{9 \\sqrt{3}}{2})$."} {:answer/id #uuid "0db23885-cc2d-5561-87f8-938cc3c7822e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{9 \\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722446} {:answer/id #uuid "30a08539-7656-50a9-b241-c93e5316f442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722447} {:answer/id #uuid "46bf4545-f088-53a4-a0ff-c82af9c31a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722449} {:answer/id #uuid "626ed49b-617b-5148-a562-cc76e9ae4b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722452} {:answer/id #uuid "9de78c3b-b493-5cd8-bed7-ba95e6631840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},- \\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db23885-cc2d-5561-87f8-938cc3c7822e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{9 \\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722446} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb9034a-8a14-59ae-b3c2-fc43210e05fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(9 \\sqrt{3},9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722448} {:answer/id #uuid "46bf4545-f088-53a4-a0ff-c82af9c31a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722449} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9f365b-ceee-50fd-9f89-0922276e179b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{9 \\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{9}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722466} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e41b1d-b579-5a7c-934b-ee390f808ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722468} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d631005-5c1b-55d0-858e-abefb09ea2b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ab50779e-bc43-5836-b576-30b711858336" :question/problem "![](images/28/2879a21b84b281a2578aed1a687bfb864df7017bbb55eb87bcc735c722fd5f12.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(9,60^\\circ)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=15^\\circ$.\n\nUse the marked arc and the positions of $A$ and $B$ to decide whether to increase or decrease the angle of $A$.\n\nWhich ordered pair gives the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $9$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is clockwise; subtract the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=60^\\circ-15^\\circ=45^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(9,45^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=9\\cos\\left(45^\\circ\\right)=9\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{2},\\\\y &=9\\sin\\left(45^\\circ\\right)=9\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{2})$."} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9f365b-ceee-50fd-9f89-0922276e179b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{9 \\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{9}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722466} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e41b1d-b579-5a7c-934b-ee390f808ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722468} {:answer/id #uuid "db0ce8f2-765c-5476-bdc6-986f752360e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f6fbee4-ac3e-5e76-8028-c045ff6e6951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{51}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722671} {:answer/id #uuid "45d72834-5993-5afe-b802-ca8646abe1b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722673} {:answer/id #uuid "49c0564e-9dbe-5ecf-97a4-1b4eff406746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 - 8 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722674} {:answer/id #uuid "bab31373-f1c4-538d-b434-6d2d0bc8fa22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3 + 8 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722684} {:answer/id #uuid "e124677c-f99c-5d9b-81a3-dc8826581b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722690}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722674} :answer-field/id #uuid "f516f37d-62dd-5659-8866-7cdd126d1edb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "123157f0-3b59-5f74-868f-2fa97f1e8e06" :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/aa/aa7d3041bfe9f1ccf260ecc694ffcd5084e8b00bd71482ecde611e383d4682cf.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+(t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le 2$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 4 x)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 y^{2})\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=\\frac{1}{t},\\ y=t$, $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le 2$. It runs from $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ to $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}},1)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation, with direction $(1,\\frac{1}{t^{2}})$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/67/670abe5e761fb3e80045bf6cf153979daa6ce099ecca125621749d794dcc534d.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=- 4 \\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)=- \\frac{4}{t} \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=2 \\left(t\\right)^{2}=2 t^{2} \\\\\nx'&=- \\frac{1}{t^{2}} \\\\\ny'&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise normal permits the flux formula directly.\n\nAt positive endpoints use $\\ln(1/u)=-\\ln u$, $\\ln u-\\ln v=\\ln(u/v)$, and $\\ln e=1$ as needed; retain the logarithm terms in the final value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}\\left[\\left(- \\frac{4}{t}\\right)(1)-(2 t^{2})\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}(2 - \\frac{4}{t})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[2 t - 4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2} \\\\\n&=(4 - 4 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)})-(1 + 4 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}) \\\\\n&=3 - 8 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57bbe087-7b12-5484-b19b-fe8ee4b5b6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722677} {:answer/id #uuid "d62e5cbb-4868-5038-b8b6-245d349702d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722688} {:answer/id #uuid "e108f8a3-f1b1-5719-ad5a-4e9a4d53630c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 e - \\frac{4}{e} + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722689} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8b5258-8834-5a34-8f5d-de3d5b36164e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722694} {:answer/id #uuid "fcee4766-ab1e-5d0c-8f12-30176b7b8850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722695} :answer-field/id #uuid "d45dc882-586a-5202-b47b-8db300de9c04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "40aad8db-170b-5694-ba59-3625a80df439" :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/5b/5b30c80b806551a0945d42168bca59d027f13138d6d2b8ea7ae6338a4c4e8d28.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf i+(2 t)\\,\\mathbf j$, $1\\le t\\le e$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(4 e^{- x})\\,\\mathbf i+(-1)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)},\\ y=2 t$, $1\\le t\\le e$. It runs from $(0,2)$ to $(1,2 e)$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(\\frac{1}{t},2)$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation, with direction $(2,- \\frac{1}{t})$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/e0/e09337a70ae1b90db8ae5b05c10b77ce4e6c57ccd071a9859ada26da55909891.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=4 e^{- \\left(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}=\\frac{4}{t} \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=-1 \\\\\nx'&=\\frac{1}{t} \\\\\ny'&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise normal permits the flux formula directly.\n\nAt positive endpoints use $\\ln(1/u)=-\\ln u$, $\\ln u-\\ln v=\\ln(u/v)$, and $\\ln e=1$ as needed; retain the logarithm terms in the final value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{1}^{e}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{e}\\left[\\left(\\frac{4}{t}\\right)(2)-(-1)\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{e}\\left(\\frac{9}{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[9 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{1}^{e} \\\\\n&=(9)-(0) \\\\\n&=9\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100ef981-aaa9-5a4f-afb2-3ac5661558ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722661} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea1bba4-d936-5f5f-bfa2-e78086a468b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722668} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5fc286-c9a6-50bc-b64f-d27fa8f58ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722669} {:answer/id #uuid "b39f1d71-0824-52dc-b800-41fe8c12a37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722683} {:answer/id #uuid "c433de28-7808-5975-9549-e7a324db6144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722685}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722661} :answer-field/id #uuid "232e239e-26ed-5062-a5d4-138d7bb4b86c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f261d926-f97d-5138-85a4-bc88db4758e0" :question/problem "![Curve and selected normal](images/ba/ba1497413ac0f51a0b3f74bacb325a5c73ddf3166cde4be09d1d5b7bcd010b7b.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=(t)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$, $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le 2$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(4)\\,\\mathbf i+(2)\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown. Gray field arrows indicate direction only." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied parameterization $x=t,\\ y=\\frac{1}{t}$, $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le 2$. It runs from $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ to $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$.\n\nThe tangent has direction $(1,- \\frac{1}{t^{2}})$. The pictured normal is its $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise rotation, with direction $(\\frac{1}{t^{2}},1)$.\n\n![Tangent and selected normal](images/d7/d7ea4aed5cad1335458a52c27f75f98e93ab7c3e66ec85a809399e23465aead0.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into the field and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x(t),y(t))&=4 \\\\\nQ(x(t),y(t))&=2 \\\\\nx'&=1 \\\\\ny'&=- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe clockwise formula has the opposite normal, so negate it for the selected normal.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds&=-\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}(Py\\prime-Qx\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=-\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}\\left[(4)\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)-(2)(1)\\right]\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2}(2 + \\frac{4}{t^{2}})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\\n&=\\left[2 t - \\frac{4}{t}\\right]_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{2} \\\\\n&=(2)-(-7) \\\\\n&=9\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6b2cb3-0953-5e99-95be-7f2e3934e3c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}+\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2},\\,\\frac{2}{t+1}-2e^{2t},\\,-2\\sin(2t)-\\cos(2t)\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722855} {:answer/id #uuid "58979c92-b2f5-538f-b4fe-588be7a90ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2},\\,\\frac{2}{t+1}-2e^{2t},\\,-4\\sin(2t)-2\\cos(2t)\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d6b2cb3-0953-5e99-95be-7f2e3934e3c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}+\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2},\\,\\frac{2}{t+1}-2e^{2t},\\,-2\\sin(2t)-\\cos(2t)\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722855} {:answer/id #uuid "58979c92-b2f5-538f-b4fe-588be7a90ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2},\\,\\frac{2}{t+1}-2e^{2t},\\,-4\\sin(2t)-2\\cos(2t)\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722860} {:answer/id #uuid "88847e9f-a422-57a9-8b73-226199df0502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}+\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2},\\,\\frac{2}{t+1}-2e^{2t},\\,-4\\sin(2t)+2\\cos(2t)\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722863} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3f9aaf-3f27-59c2-a3d6-fb20b479677f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}+\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2},\\,\\frac{2}{t+1}-e^{2t},\\,-4\\sin(2t)-2\\cos(2t)\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722867} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d6a4b0-2759-50be-913c-14168089897e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}+\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2},\\,\\frac{2}{t+1}-2e^{2t},\\,-4\\sin(2t)-2\\cos(2t)\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722868}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722868} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7d6cfd6-1cb8-54ba-b98c-27b5ac880619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "87c2dd6b-a359-5e66-9212-aad50d85ddce" :question/problem "For $t>-1$, find $(2\\mathbf f-\\mathbf g)\\prime(t)$ in component form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf f(t)&=\\langle \\sqrt{t+1},\\,\\ln(t+1),\\,\\cos(2t)\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf g(t)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{t+1},\\,e^{2t},\\,\\sin(2t)\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Keep the constant coefficients while differentiating:\n\n$$\n(2\\mathbf f-\\mathbf g)\\prime(t)=2\\mathbf f\\prime(t)-\\mathbf g\\prime(t).\n$$\n\nThe component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf f\\prime(t)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t+1}},\\,\\frac{1}{t+1},\\,-2\\sin(2t)\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf g\\prime(t)&=\\langle -\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2},\\,2e^{2t},\\,2\\cos(2t)\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factors of $2$ in the exponential and trigonometric derivatives come from differentiating their inner argument $2t$. Now scale and subtract component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\mathbf f\\prime(t)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}},\\,\\frac{2}{t+1},\\,-4\\sin(2t)\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting $\\mathbf g\\prime(t)$ gives $(2\\mathbf f-\\mathbf g)\\prime(t)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}+\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{t+1}-2e^{2t} \\\\ -4\\sin(2t)-2\\cos(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe first component contains a plus sign because the reciprocal derivative is negative and is being subtracted. The stated domain keeps both the logarithm and all derivative components defined."} {:answer/id #uuid "88847e9f-a422-57a9-8b73-226199df0502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}+\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2},\\,\\frac{2}{t+1}-2e^{2t},\\,-4\\sin(2t)+2\\cos(2t)\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722863} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3f9aaf-3f27-59c2-a3d6-fb20b479677f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}+\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2},\\,\\frac{2}{t+1}-e^{2t},\\,-4\\sin(2t)-2\\cos(2t)\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722867} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d6a4b0-2759-50be-913c-14168089897e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}+\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2},\\,\\frac{2}{t+1}-2e^{2t},\\,-4\\sin(2t)-2\\cos(2t)\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "180175f0-8773-506b-8b00-8d8c0749b40d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 2,3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723000} {:answer/id #uuid "1a87165d-6644-5910-8ae6-e6c699a15409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 3,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723001} {:answer/id #uuid "588d4db8-97b0-5154-a824-f43dcee28ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 3,4\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723009} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8eb4ba-d9b9-5a35-9673-abf4584d07c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 3,2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723014} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c81500-fb2b-5024-8e41-82d055a7e35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 0,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723027}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723014} :answer-field/id #uuid "a678c021-1118-5f75-9554-d3a0acc7f8f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "26cb07bf-6d97-557d-869e-11d3463db4a9" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=\\ln{\\left(3 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 3 \\right)}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through $P=(1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other coordinate constant when taking each partial derivative. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=\\frac{6 x}{3 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 3} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{4 y}{3 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 3} \\\\ \\nabla f(1,1)&=\\langle \\frac{3}{4},\\frac{1}{2}\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it is **normal** to the tangent line. Its dot product with the displacement along the line is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(1,1)\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle&=0 \\\\ \\frac{3 x}{4} + \\frac{y}{2} - \\frac{5}{4}&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying or dividing the whole equation by a nonzero constant preserves the line. Thus the tangent equation is\n\n$$\n\\langle 3,2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d0e855e-485a-5ae6-83e0-4c4e6b9ec141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723199} {:answer/id #uuid "240f0aa1-435b-56a9-9312-fc2e5113c2e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723202} {:answer/id #uuid "87f05cd1-c320-510a-98e5-7cede7db50f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\,\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723211} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a70d84-bf3b-56c4-9b00-f40b9e17e80b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723215} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1c7dc6-008c-571b-b3c7-efd7e1bb35a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\mathbf i+\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723202} :answer-field/id #uuid "da331a3e-78c1-5a2f-8633-0c82909dad28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1e3f745c-e668-5837-86c3-dcb8518e8128" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}=x - 3 \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)} - \\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms to one side and set $f(x,y)=- x + 3 \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$. The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, and $f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ confirms that the point lies on it.\n\nA tangent vector is perpendicular to the nonzero gradient. Use $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j$. Differentiate while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=3 y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - 3 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 1\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=3 x \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - 3 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the supplied point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)&=-1\\\\ &\\quad - 3 \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\\\ &\\quad +3 \\left(0\\right) \\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\\\ &=-4\\\\ f_y(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}\\\\ &\\quad +3 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\\\ &=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus one tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)\\cdot\\mathbf t(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)&=\\left(-4\\right)\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)\\\\ &\\quad-\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)\\left(-4\\right)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple has the same tangent direction; none of the other listed vectors is perpendicular to this gradient."} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1d0969-7569-5abc-8d6f-f70c19bfa306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723235} {:answer/id #uuid "f64f7050-8ade-5153-973d-255f478e5001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723259} :answer-field/id #uuid "6820da9c-9b6c-54f2-bcd2-0e5baf2bbdc2" :answer-field/key "$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\langle-\\frac{y}{4}+\\cos(x^2),\\;\\frac{3x}{4}+y^3\\rangle$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723243} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54adae31-d756-520d-8fa7-8cfccf7f4a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723240} {:answer/id #uuid "de4c4b12-afc3-5de8-b46f-dca9f4961854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723255} :answer-field/id #uuid "f221ac14-ad8c-54c3-bf4b-f7fe8a6eff11" :answer-field/key "$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\langle ye^{xy}-\\frac{y}{2},\\;xe^{xy}+\\frac{x}{2}\\rangle$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0dc03b8-25f8-5532-b817-8cae60415fd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724444}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724444} :answer-field/id #uuid "51bbc5bd-a8d0-535d-8bb5-8d0ccea55e9f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4444bc8b-40ea-5381-bb96-09edd4083790" :question/problem "The lines $y=4x-1$ and $-3kx-8y=-5$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k$?\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For two lines with finite slopes to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be $-1$.\n\nThe line $y=4x-1$ is in slope-intercept form, so its slope is $4$.\n\nConvert $-3kx-8y=-5$ to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3kx-8y &=-5 \\\\ -8y &=3kx-5 \\\\ y &=-\\frac{3}{8}kx+\\frac{5}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts slope is $-\\frac{3}{8}k$.\n\nMultiply the slopes and solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8}k) &=-1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}k &=-1 \\\\ k &=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8af38a41-6266-5d45-97fb-ba96f788f8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724441}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724441} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a7fd2b3-84b9-57a7-9ae4-21f7d2d55b0d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5c1936d3-afa4-5b9c-8a17-392309649c28" :question/problem "The lines $-3y-4kx=8$ and $y=5x+7$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k$?\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For two lines with finite slopes to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be $-1$.\n\nConvert $-3y-4kx=8$ to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3y-4kx &=8 \\\\ -3y &=4kx+8 \\\\ y &=-\\frac{4}{3}kx-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts slope is $-\\frac{4}{3}k$.\n\nThe line $y=5x+7$ is in slope-intercept form, so its slope is $5$.\n\nMultiply the slopes and solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{4}{3}k)\\cdot (5) &=-1 \\\\ -\\frac{20}{3}k &=-1 \\\\ k &=\\frac{3}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0aa0898-cbc9-5c47-8fc3-2d97292579bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724524} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eef2eff-d554-5c89-a5c4-d6c11036fb1e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fc7f9bf9-f95e-5cc6-bf0c-2e19428f6c09" :question/problem "The lines $-8y-3x=2$ and $-4qx+8y=5$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $q$?\n\n$q=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For two lines with finite slopes to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be $-1$.\n\nConvert $-8y-3x=2$ to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8y-3x &=2 \\\\ -8y &=3x+2 \\\\ y &=-\\frac{3}{8}x-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts slope is $-\\frac{3}{8}$.\n\nConvert $-4qx+8y=5$ to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4qx+8y &=5 \\\\ 8y &=4qx+5 \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2}qx+\\frac{5}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts slope is $\\frac{1}{2}q$.\n\nMultiply the slopes and solve for $q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(-\\frac{3}{8}\\right)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}q) &=-1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{16}q &=-1 \\\\ q &=\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "65380eec-e05d-51f2-9630-fe4a522dfa7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},- \\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725193} {:answer/id #uuid "67ca7f54-1b18-52a9-a569-a948a77d991b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725194} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb20805-8d63-582e-8b24-9f4b45c389f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725195} {:answer/id #uuid "88538f6a-e1ac-5216-add9-628a0a586a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65380eec-e05d-51f2-9630-fe4a522dfa7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},- \\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725193} {:answer/id #uuid "67ca7f54-1b18-52a9-a569-a948a77d991b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725194} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb20805-8d63-582e-8b24-9f4b45c389f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725195} {:answer/id #uuid "88538f6a-e1ac-5216-add9-628a0a586a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725196} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe02da4-9852-50e8-8291-a078ca30615b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,- 3 \\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725193} :answer-field/id #uuid "54b7dc96-87eb-55fc-898e-83d6387a3d02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fbfc6913-0e92-53fc-b281-babc7cfb3db6" :question/problem "![](images/84/84d563cdb67d07391e980881cf68aea58ffe98b678f045530aabae6adbbabc79.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(3,240^\\circ)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=30^\\circ$.\n\nUse the marked arc and the positions of $A$ and $B$ to decide whether to increase or decrease the angle of $A$.\n\nWhich ordered pair gives the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $3$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is clockwise; subtract the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=240^\\circ-30^\\circ=210^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(3,210^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=3\\cos\\left(210^\\circ\\right)=3\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},\\\\y &=3\\sin\\left(210^\\circ\\right)=3\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=- \\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},- \\frac{3}{2})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38aa6a1e-aa30-5323-94d2-682a4c47ec57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(7 \\sqrt{3},-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725210} {:answer/id #uuid "6a10678c-a8c9-5974-9940-257d775105de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725211} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e92588-f40a-5106-bdd5-0ed98004c5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2},- \\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725212} {:answer/id #uuid "b29d905f-fc21-506e-b87f-6698b7472f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725213} {:answer/id #uuid "ec86aa6c-0086-5abf-94e8-b2f9b3d98341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{2},- \\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725215}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725212} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6006b39-6277-568a-a006-0cd23ce5309f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "10fcbb53-1adb-5e77-97c5-4b40ae5e2a40" :question/problem "![](images/81/812ca42ad6f3b14494f3a4118211e4b25a7930c41910f8613a22e03e52af5cd3.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(7,315^\\circ)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=15^\\circ$.\n\nMoving from $A$ to $B$ along the marked arc is counterclockwise, so add the marked angle. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$ to find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nWhich ordered pair gives the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $7$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is counterclockwise; add the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=315^\\circ+15^\\circ=330^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(7,330^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=7\\cos\\left(330^\\circ\\right)=7\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2},\\\\y &=7\\sin\\left(330^\\circ\\right)=7\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=- \\frac{7}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2},- \\frac{7}{2})$."} {:answer/id #uuid "6a10678c-a8c9-5974-9940-257d775105de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725211} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e92588-f40a-5106-bdd5-0ed98004c5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2},- \\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725212} {:answer/id #uuid "b29d905f-fc21-506e-b87f-6698b7472f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725213} {:answer/id #uuid "ec86aa6c-0086-5abf-94e8-b2f9b3d98341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{2},- \\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186730283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ab3e605-8b60-51eb-ab77-d7ba3a507b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186730280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186730280} :answer-field/id #uuid "2da75709-efcd-5e1d-8de6-95f01cd0d8a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186730274} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6ea7e78-0f1d-5a28-bd3a-59a0992de8e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186730286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186730286} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab9a817a-804b-5f6a-869e-4774f9877538" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186730282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b0264cae-5a57-5c94-b40e-0d82990eeed0" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\csc\\left(- \\frac{\\pi x^{3}}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nat $(1, - \\frac{1}{2})$.\n\n$y =$ {{field-1}} $x +$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First identify the inner function and differentiate each layer.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= - \\frac{\\pi x^{3}}{2} \\\\\nu'(x) &= - \\frac{3 \\pi x^{2}}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\csc\\left(u\\right)\\right) &= - \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\csc\\left(u\\right)\\cot\\left(u\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by the inner derivative and substitute the original argument.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x) &= \\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\csc\\left(u\\right)\\cot\\left(u\\right)\\right)\\left(- \\frac{3 \\pi x^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\\nf'(x) &= \\frac{3 \\pi x^{2}}{4}\\,\\csc\\left(- \\frac{\\pi x^{3}}{2}\\right)\\cot\\left(- \\frac{\\pi x^{3}}{2}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the argument, inner derivative, and trigonometric factors at the specified point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(1) &= - \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\\\\nu'(1) &= - \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) &= -1 \\\\\n\\cot\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm = f'(1) &= \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\,\\left(-1\\right)\\left(0\\right) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the point and slope to find the intercept.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= y_0-mx_0 \\\\\nb &= - \\frac{1}{2} - \\left(0\\right)\\left(1\\right) \\\\\nb &= - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186730285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0a2a923-f0cc-593c-93c9-0537a89ec645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + 5 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186730281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186730281} :answer-field/id #uuid "406be120-faa1-52ea-8091-e97949c45308" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186730276} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8e5cab0-36b2-5c11-8cd6-f8706f006415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 5 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186730287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186730287} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e011df7-522c-5bcb-a4a9-d6d5c28e5a02" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186730277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e3ccb527-3c94-5b65-8e08-8f2b5dfbfd0f" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\cot\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi x^{3}}{6}\\right)\n$$\n\nat $(1, - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$.\n\n$y =$ {{field-1}} $x +$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First identify the inner function and differentiate each layer.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{5 \\pi x^{3}}{6} \\\\\nu'(x) &= \\frac{5 \\pi x^{2}}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\cot\\left(u\\right)\\right) &= - \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\csc^{2}\\left(u\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by the inner derivative and substitute the original argument.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x) &= \\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\csc^{2}\\left(u\\right)\\right)\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi x^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\\nf'(x) &= - \\frac{5 \\pi x^{2}}{4}\\,\\csc^{2}\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi x^{3}}{6}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the argument, inner derivative, and trigonometric factors at the specified point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(1) &= \\frac{5 \\pi}{6} \\\\\nu'(1) &= \\frac{5 \\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}\\right) &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm = f'(1) &= - \\frac{5 \\pi}{4}\\,\\left(2\\right)^2 = - 5 \\pi\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the point and slope to find the intercept.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= y_0-mx_0 \\\\\nb &= - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} - \\left(- 5 \\pi\\right)\\left(1\\right) \\\\\nb &= - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + 5 \\pi \\\\\ny &= - 5 \\pi x - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + 5 \\pi\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4538e3c6-855b-5c4d-a06e-5e4ef63e7301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731404} {:answer/id #uuid "eefb147f-5b89-5d57-8a95-3958ed84e5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731435}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731435} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c9b7680-d1ef-5d43-8a17-d54e41998e07" :answer-field/key "**III.** The graph passes through $(\\frac{\\pi}{2},1)$." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "981e7897-d705-5070-80b0-e949ddb4b512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}+(\\ln(x)-1)\\tan(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731612} :answer-field/id #uuid "4820ef98-4fb3-5c53-bea8-4493d69d3c11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7841967c-972b-56df-b39e-4be5cd455a30" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ if $y=(\\ln(x)-1)\\sec(x)$.\n\n$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\sec(x)($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Apply the product rule; the derivative of the constant shift is zero. Factor secant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\sec(x)+(\\ln(x)-1)\\sec(x)\\tan(x) \\\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\sec(x)(\\frac{1}{x}+(\\ln(x)-1)\\tan(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe requested fragment is $\\frac{1}{x}+(\\ln(x)-1)\\tan(x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731645 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fce08b46-8be7-5c1f-8f4d-1269c98537ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3} \\pi}{3} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731652}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731652} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9145d74-8720-5682-a88d-f726f40d81fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a68ffee5-71c9-5b78-b7c1-9767950118b5" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=-x\\sec(x)$ at $x=-\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Give an exact value.\n\n$\\left. \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\right|_{x=-\\frac{\\pi}{3}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First differentiate, then substitute the specified point into the derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =-1\\sec(x)-x(\\sec(x)\\tan(x)) \\\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =-\\sec(x)(1+x\\tan(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=-\\frac{\\pi}{3}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y'\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) & =-\\sec\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)(1+\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\tan\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)) \\\\ y'\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) & =-(2)(1+\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)(- \\sqrt{3})) \\\\ y'\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) & =- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3} \\pi}{3} - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e522b34f-4f32-518a-9a18-59921306cf37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(-4 - \\pi\\right)}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731651} :answer-field/id #uuid "d78e9286-2e8e-5d5a-a1b9-81e6f3dd2896" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "acd0f3a4-1aa0-5a74-8915-2950a4355258" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=-x\\sec(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$. Give an exact value.\n\n$\\left. \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\right|_{x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First differentiate, then substitute the specified point into the derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =-1\\sec(x)-x(\\sec(x)\\tan(x)) \\\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =-\\sec(x)(1+x\\tan(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y'\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) & =-\\sec\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)(1+\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)) \\\\ y'\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) & =-(\\sqrt{2})(1+\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)(1)) \\\\ y'\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(-4 - \\pi\\right)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6a29707-9d65-5340-9f8c-c94a894e7753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{8 \\sqrt{3} \\pi}{3} - 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731717}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731717} :answer-field/id #uuid "3014487b-5541-5d44-ad8f-d9e1adf81898" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d26ee6ae-a440-522d-85ff-6f9f6cba0372" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=-4x\\sec(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Give an exact value.\n\n$\\left. \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\right|_{x=\\frac{\\pi}{3}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First differentiate, then substitute the specified point into the derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =-4\\cdot 1\\sec(x)-4x(\\sec(x)\\tan(x)) \\\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =-4\\sec(x)(1+x\\tan(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y'\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) & =-4\\sec\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)(1+\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)) \\\\ y'\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) & =-4(2)(1+\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\ y'\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) & =- \\frac{8 \\sqrt{3} \\pi}{3} - 8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dd4c731-359b-5f13-998f-e8a660521b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6250 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731967} {:answer/id #uuid "1037649b-16b9-5c7a-b59d-86819cfc80cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3125 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731968} {:answer/id #uuid "38bbd9be-f619-5a99-bb3d-1b1b06c66fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3125 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731974} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5910c1-3911-5943-8f2e-1ff9558a4626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "125 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731978} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6a6ef0-07a4-5db6-a334-c936724364aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6250 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731993}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731968} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f733b7d-3317-58f9-84da-0450922b8919" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "33496273-64d8-5e5a-abd6-98fc7e1fa3bb" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n\\oint_C F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\n where $F(x,y)=\\langle 4^x-\\frac{5}{3}y^3+2,\\frac{2}{y^2+1}+\\frac{5}{3}x^3\\rangle$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=25,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The boundary is positively oriented, and $P,Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=4^x-\\frac{5}{3}y^3+2, \\\\ Q(x,y)&=\\frac{2}{y^2+1}+\\frac{5}{3}x^3, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=- 5 y^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=5 x^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the cross-partial subtraction is\n\n$$\nQ_x-P_y=(5 x^{2})-(- 5 y^{2})=5 x^{2} + 5 y^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe enclosed region has polar bounds\n\n$$\n0\\le r\\le 5,\\qquad 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi.\n$$\n\n![](images/9f/9f86c721572a1017402adabf42b23e4193e24414b31ed3ba711ef1d2c75e581f.png)\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, together with\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nLet $I=\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy$. Evaluating the radial and angular integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I&=\\iint_D(5 x^{2} + 5 y^{2})\\,dA \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{5}5r^3\\left(1\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{5}\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}1\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\left(\\frac{625-0}{4}\\right)\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\frac{3125}{4}\\left((2 \\pi)-(0)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{3125}{4}\\left(2 \\pi\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{3125 \\pi}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a993608-3eca-5258-86ae-825f029eb4a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731969} {:answer/id #uuid "39cdd3dd-adbc-500b-8dbd-9e9b5aa8a3e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731975} {:answer/id #uuid "54933dd3-150d-569d-9991-118f439be4b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "81 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731979} {:answer/id #uuid "54be7f7b-1f81-5621-a5be-9f36c9dec271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "324 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731980} {:answer/id #uuid "f2736bb5-fb76-53c4-b4cc-a80e67eb140e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "108 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731998}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731979} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd829c2-9f63-56a3-b2e9-e01fccbe6bb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f7fa67c-5f14-5593-89a8-5af95cb1f0a5" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n\\oint_C F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\n where $F(x,y)=\\langle \\sin^3 x-\\frac{2}{3}y^3,y^4+\\frac{2}{3}x^3\\rangle$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=9,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The boundary is positively oriented, and $P,Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=\\sin^3 x-\\frac{2}{3}y^3, \\\\ Q(x,y)&=y^4+\\frac{2}{3}x^3, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=- 2 y^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=2 x^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the cross-partial subtraction is\n\n$$\nQ_x-P_y=(2 x^{2})-(- 2 y^{2})=2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe enclosed region has polar bounds\n\n$$\n0\\le r\\le 3,\\qquad 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi.\n$$\n\n![](images/6f/6f7b5381fa7792a722e515878be7e2a964d85b9e7f5a17aa83b3983b7f2a7bf5.png)\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, together with\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nLet $I=\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy$. Evaluating the radial and angular integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I&=\\iint_D(2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,dA \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{3}2r^3\\left(1\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=2\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}1\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=2\\left(\\frac{81-0}{4}\\right)\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\frac{81}{2}\\left((2 \\pi)-(0)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{81}{2}\\left(2 \\pi\\right) \\\\ &=81 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f3f9b6-d767-5282-940b-3d922f47b003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731973} {:answer/id #uuid "a425104c-a2ec-548c-8914-ea812806ffba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731990} {:answer/id #uuid "a80efa57-9279-5e5a-a036-6042a6255a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731991} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2580b0-f65d-59f1-96e0-e61b62426ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731996} {:answer/id #uuid "ede1fdc2-e09b-55aa-9426-6319b502ad2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\sqrt{2} \\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731997}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731991} :answer-field/id #uuid "0286abaa-0082-5f28-a2ae-e55c5baa31e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186731964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "78eea50a-2bc3-58bc-8d3d-5c70f97289d3" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n\\oint_C F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\n where $F(x,y)=\\langle \\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}-y^3}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{y+5}+x^3}{3}\\rangle$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=2,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The boundary is positively oriented, and $P,Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}-y^3}{3}, \\\\ Q(x,y)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{y+5}+x^3}{3}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=- y^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=x^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the cross-partial subtraction is\n\n$$\nQ_x-P_y=(x^{2})-(- y^{2})=x^{2} + y^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe enclosed region has polar bounds\n\n$$\n0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\qquad 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi.\n$$\n\n![](images/05/05e4bafff74735452cbc481853d2de4ddf42f207fd52bf8a45f53382dbe20f5c.png)\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, together with\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nLet $I=\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy$. Evaluating the radial and angular integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I&=\\iint_D(x^{2} + y^{2})\\,dA \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}1r^3\\left(1\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=1\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}1\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{4-0}{4}\\right)\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=1\\left((2 \\pi)-(0)\\right) \\\\ &=1\\left(2 \\pi\\right) \\\\ &=2 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732080 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36304658-c15a-5c00-8f9d-517da0b54639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=- 2 x + \\frac{7}{e} + 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732077} {:answer/id #uuid "45526fed-265f-5cf5-ab12-a191e751d933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{1}{2 e} + \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732083} {:answer/id #uuid "e783f5c9-1e69-5055-9070-69170f7728d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2 x - 1 - e^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732100} {:answer/id #uuid "f1de4da2-ac8c-513e-b9ce-7bb833323a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=- \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{4}{e} + 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732102} {:answer/id #uuid "fae80af1-98bc-5ebf-b03b-6cd11b853c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=- 2 x + \\frac{8}{e} + 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732104}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732077} :answer-field/id #uuid "23c6dc5c-75c6-5832-bae3-a67e140a00d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3a28badc-511c-5be7-a0e1-11e984da0662" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=2 e^{t} + 2,\\\\y&=- 2 t e^{t} + e^{t} + 3,\\\\&-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point where $t=-1$. Find the Cartesian equation of the normal to the curve at $C$. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $t=-1$ into both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_0&=2 e^{(-1)} + 2=\\frac{2}{e} + 2,\\\\y_0&=- 2 (-1) e^{(-1)} + e^{(-1)} + 3=\\frac{3}{e} + 3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $e^{-1}=1/e$ when evaluating the coordinates and derivatives.\n\nDifferentiate the coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=2 e^{t},\\\\y^{\\prime}(t)&=- 2 t e^{t} - e^{t}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product rule gives $y^{\\prime}(t)=- 2 e^{t}- 2 t e^{t}+e^{t}$ before collecting terms.\n\nForm the tangent derivative ratio:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=\\frac{y^{\\prime}(t)}{x^{\\prime}(t)},\\\\&=\\frac{- 2 t e^{t} - e^{t}}{2 e^{t}},\\\\&=- t - \\frac{1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=-1$, the tangent slope is\n\n$$\nm=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{t=-1}=- (-1) - \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTake the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{2}}=-2.\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $C=(\\frac{2}{e} + 2,\\frac{3}{e} + 3)$, distribute, and collect constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(\\frac{3}{e} + 3)&=-2(x-(\\frac{2}{e} + 2)),\\\\y&=- 2 x+\\frac{4}{e} + 4+\\frac{3}{e} + 3,\\\\y&=- 2 x + \\frac{7}{e} + 7.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e258f6-3f89-53cd-963e-f2fc531b0d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732319} {:answer/id #uuid "66db7637-b533-5e11-b92d-4e9ea332e35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732335} {:answer/id #uuid "94a5b407-2c29-5e84-8389-e328121f4d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2 - \\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732340} {:answer/id #uuid "cc36f850-9c76-5580-96ed-b409a55659c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2 + \\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732346} {:answer/id #uuid "f528ee6f-fd64-5580-a584-2289057587ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732352}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732319} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f38f204-7fc6-5411-8237-6b8504bd0e2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "81f077e6-28e8-5957-bcdd-06719e801647" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=- 2 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$ at the point $A(-1,e)$ in the direction towards the point $B(2,e)$." :question/worked-solution "Use a unit direction vector to measure change per unit distance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(A)&=\\nabla f(A)\\cdot\\mathbf u, \\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\frac{\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst find the unit direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf v&=\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\langle 2,e\\rangle-\\langle -1,e\\rangle=\\langle 3,0\\rangle \\\\\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{(3)^2+(0)^2}=3 \\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\frac{1}{3}\\langle 3,0\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCompute both partial derivatives, holding the other variable fixed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=- 2 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)} \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=- \\frac{2 x}{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at the starting point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(-1,e)&=-2 \\\\\nf_y(-1,e)&=\\frac{2}{e} \\\\\n\\nabla f(-1,e)&=\\langle -2,\\frac{2}{e}\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product and simplify the exact value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(-1,e)&=\\langle -2,\\frac{2}{e}\\rangle\\cdot\\frac{1}{3}\\langle 3,0\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\frac{(-2)(3)+\\left(\\frac{2}{e}\\right)(0)}{3} \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fce9625-b5a5-5c76-888d-1f66cae80673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732325} {:answer/id #uuid "3708e5b9-24dc-522b-a248-ec6ffee509d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{22 \\sqrt{5}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732328} {:answer/id #uuid "46b6812a-4503-5020-824f-1bb434b9f543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{22 \\sqrt{5}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732329} {:answer/id #uuid "5c8e92ab-b160-516f-9c14-761405ec3586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732332} {:answer/id #uuid "af1507ef-99ad-52fe-b788-7c7a52725b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732325} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fb45b8a-fedf-5d6a-b150-c9daac4d3fad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a4d7dbfc-684c-53b7-b696-610ba4605637" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=- 2 \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}$ at the point $A(-4,3)$ in the direction towards the point $B(-2,4)$." :question/worked-solution "Use a unit direction vector to measure change per unit distance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(A)&=\\nabla f(A)\\cdot\\mathbf u, \\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\frac{\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst find the unit direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf v&=\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\langle -2,4\\rangle-\\langle -4,3\\rangle=\\langle 2,1\\rangle \\\\\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{(2)^2+(1)^2}=\\sqrt{5} \\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\langle 2,1\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the chain rule to both partials, holding the other variable fixed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=- \\frac{2 x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}} \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=- \\frac{2 y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at the starting point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(-4,3)&=\\frac{8}{5} \\\\\nf_y(-4,3)&=- \\frac{6}{5} \\\\\n\\nabla f(-4,3)&=\\langle \\frac{8}{5},- \\frac{6}{5}\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product and simplify the exact value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(-4,3)&=\\langle \\frac{8}{5},- \\frac{6}{5}\\rangle\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\langle 2,1\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right)(2)+\\left(- \\frac{6}{5}\\right)(1)}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\\n&=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4320690a-80bc-585f-b242-4ab183ead20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{13}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732406}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732406} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1f4831a-3263-524b-b721-77721bc31b8e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "16e84da1-50af-529f-b2d9-2399b2870ed6" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2} - y}{x - y}$ at the point $A(2,0)$ in the direction towards the point $B(-1,2)$.\n\nThe required directional derivative is {{field-1}}. Give an exact value." :question/worked-solution "Use a unit direction vector to measure change per unit distance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(A)&=\\nabla f(A)\\cdot\\mathbf u, \\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\frac{\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst find the unit direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf v&=\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\langle -1,2\\rangle-\\langle 2,0\\rangle=\\langle -3,2\\rangle \\\\\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+(2)^2}=\\sqrt{13} \\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\langle -3,2\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe denominator at $A$ is $2\\ne0$, so the function is differentiable near $A$. Apply the quotient rule to each partial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{(2 x)(x - y)-(x^{2} - y)}{(x - y)^2} \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\frac{(-1)(x - y)-(-1)(x^{2} - y)}{(x - y)^2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at the starting point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(2,0)&=1 \\\\\nf_y(2,0)&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\nabla f(2,0)&=\\langle 1,\\frac{1}{2}\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product and simplify the exact value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(2,0)&=\\langle 1,\\frac{1}{2}\\rangle\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\langle -3,2\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\frac{(1)(-3)+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(2)}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\\n&=- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{13}}{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a812345-1211-5455-8c63-8f3c6327343e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732407}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732407} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cb37aaf-5766-5996-9e91-61cb1c2ff6a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8d5181e4-19c0-5f65-be61-cff2391522be" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2} + 2 y}{x - y}$ at the point $A(-1,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(-3,1)$.\n\nThe required directional derivative is {{field-1}}. Give an exact value." :question/worked-solution "Use a unit direction vector to measure change per unit distance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(A)&=\\nabla f(A)\\cdot\\mathbf u, \\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\frac{\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst find the unit direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf v&=\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\langle -3,1\\rangle-\\langle -1,1\\rangle=\\langle -2,0\\rangle \\\\\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{(-2)^2+(0)^2}=2 \\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\frac{1}{2}\\langle -2,0\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe denominator at $A$ is $-2\\ne0$, so the function is differentiable near $A$. Apply the quotient rule to each partial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{(2 x)(x - y)-(x^{2} + 2 y)}{(x - y)^2} \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\frac{(2)(x - y)-(-1)(x^{2} + 2 y)}{(x - y)^2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at the starting point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(-1,1)&=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\\nf_y(-1,1)&=- \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n\\nabla f(-1,1)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{4},- \\frac{1}{4}\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product and simplify the exact value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(-1,1)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{4},- \\frac{1}{4}\\rangle\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}\\langle -2,0\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)(-2)+\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)(0)}{2} \\\\\n&=- \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "541296d8-4f38-5b46-a152-50c83da66561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{132}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732583} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2152bc-814c-5110-b9d5-9395fedca15e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{66}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732584} {:answer/id #uuid "640070d1-0061-53e7-a411-6ff8f4a4cb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{132}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732586} {:answer/id #uuid "ac44a240-ab8d-5e8c-bb6b-b033ee07d073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{174}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732599} {:answer/id #uuid "c41b600e-e202-5459-ae0b-38d7f397b77b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{132}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732602}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732583} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e400da1-c37d-535d-9e44-bfe7b75ce287" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "06cfa343-c4cb-5ab2-9cfd-bb8c4e938e6d" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} - 3 y^{2}$. What is the signed rate of change of $f$ at the point $(1,-2)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v=\\langle 24,-10\\rangle$?" :question/worked-solution "The rate is the directional derivative $D_{\\mathbf u}f=\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf u$, with $\\mathbf u$ a unit vector.\n\nCompute the partial derivatives and evaluate them at the given point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=- 6 x \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- 6 y \\\\ \\nabla f(1,-2)&=\\langle -6,12\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNormalize the direction vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{(24)^2+(-10)^2}=26 \\\\ \\mathbf u&=\\langle \\frac{12}{13},- \\frac{5}{13}\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen take the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f&=\\langle -6,12\\rangle\\cdot \\langle \\frac{12}{13},- \\frac{5}{13}\\rangle \\\\ &=\\frac{(-6)(24)+(12)(-10)}{26} \\\\ &=- \\frac{132}{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c7dbc8-d86a-51c5-99b5-5825e87bea88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732573} {:answer/id #uuid "3af12681-8b87-5dfe-91f7-a01d37807697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145} \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732577} {:answer/id #uuid "6994bd7d-bb75-5180-97ac-f3f8f77e6ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{6 \\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732588} {:answer/id #uuid "6b02bfbe-1c93-5138-bc35-2b5f2c38eceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{145} \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732589} {:answer/id #uuid "a8bc0011-9ad8-5a44-b9f0-329c8e682cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12 \\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732598}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732588} :answer-field/id #uuid "930565e8-4b1e-5a76-ac2e-47fd4a53fd80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "25a122cb-447a-53f3-9244-f6adf0fbfb08" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2 \\pi x y + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\pi x \\right)}}{y}$. What is the signed rate of change of $f$ at the point $(-2,2)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v=\\langle -4,-3\\rangle$?" :question/worked-solution "The rate is the directional derivative $D_{\\mathbf u}f=\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf u$, with $\\mathbf u$ a unit vector.\n\nCompute the partial derivatives and evaluate them at the given point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2 \\pi y + \\frac{\\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi x \\right)}}{y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=2 \\pi x - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\pi x \\right)}}{y^{2}} \\\\ \\nabla f(-2,2)&=\\langle \\frac{9 \\pi}{2},- 4 \\pi\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNormalize the direction vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{(-4)^2+(-3)^2}=5 \\\\ \\mathbf u&=\\langle - \\frac{4}{5},- \\frac{3}{5}\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen take the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f&=\\langle \\frac{9 \\pi}{2},- 4 \\pi\\rangle\\cdot \\langle - \\frac{4}{5},- \\frac{3}{5}\\rangle \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{9 \\pi}{2}\\right)(-4)+(- 4 \\pi)(-3)}{5} \\\\ &=- \\frac{6 \\pi}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "304a7e2a-b47f-580d-b151-0a2a33461611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{7 e}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732575} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7297e0-6456-552b-af69-6c05aa405536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{6 e}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732579} {:answer/id #uuid "8f02254b-6991-544a-8a9e-1b2e2356e35d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{6 e}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732594} {:answer/id #uuid "9e832206-cb7d-55d7-b659-327b03d4828f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{6 e}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732597} {:answer/id #uuid "c4281f49-d119-51e8-8ff7-e5f20d979b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6 e}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732594} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fac4572-750d-5e75-8383-7db73f1a7798" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2e3fe671-2afb-5e99-8acd-91686c0637ff" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2 e^{y}}{x y}$. What is the signed rate of change of $f$ at the point $(-2,1)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v=\\langle 12,-5\\rangle$?" :question/worked-solution "The rate is the directional derivative $D_{\\mathbf u}f=\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf u$, with $\\mathbf u$ a unit vector.\n\nCompute the partial derivatives and evaluate them at the given point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=- \\frac{2 e^{y}}{x^{2} y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{2 \\left(y - 1\\right) e^{y}}{x y^{2}} \\\\ \\nabla f(-2,1)&=\\langle - \\frac{e}{2},0\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNormalize the direction vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{(12)^2+(-5)^2}=13 \\\\ \\mathbf u&=\\langle \\frac{12}{13},- \\frac{5}{13}\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen take the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f&=\\langle - \\frac{e}{2},0\\rangle\\cdot \\langle \\frac{12}{13},- \\frac{5}{13}\\rangle \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(- \\frac{e}{2}\\right)(12)+(0)(-5)}{13} \\\\ &=- \\frac{6 e}{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732582 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "505438c9-2efa-592c-a0b9-28e3f064df12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 3 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732580} {:answer/id #uuid "522b1ff5-5210-5502-8313-1565be39216d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15 \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732581} {:answer/id #uuid "60a37420-f21a-5920-8638-91b09718fe73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 3 \\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732585} {:answer/id #uuid "740d1e26-4378-5c30-8e55-ca7583feefb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732591} {:answer/id #uuid "94b63b86-0f87-5624-ba63-8f424061f703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732596}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732591} :answer-field/id #uuid "385625f2-c1c9-5745-bfd2-7389c33fe36e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "52eef203-66a5-58e2-b53a-a0fd24c81b85" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2}}{y}$. What is the signed rate of change of $f$ at the point $(3,-1)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v=\\langle 2,-2\\rangle$?" :question/worked-solution "The rate is the directional derivative $D_{\\mathbf u}f=\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf u$, with $\\mathbf u$ a unit vector.\n\nCompute the partial derivatives and evaluate them at the given point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{2 x}{y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- \\frac{x^{2}}{y^{2}} \\\\ \\nabla f(3,-1)&=\\langle -6,-9\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNormalize the direction vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{(2)^2+(-2)^2}=2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\mathbf u&=\\langle \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen take the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f&=\\langle -6,-9\\rangle\\cdot \\langle \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\rangle \\\\ &=\\frac{(-6)(2)+(-9)(-2)}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186732601 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e9904b-2943-5399-94ca-bc8ce140ab4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186732571} {:answer/id #uuid "678e546d-f20e-5184-9810-3f1d6beb3b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218673258